28] 1\n[429] George Ridestad, the Chief Constable of Middlesex, was a man of few words, of medium height, with shrewd, piercing eyes under bushy eyebrows. He was accustomed to listening rather than talking, and then, with a voice devoid of emotion, issuing a concise command - and that command was always obeyed by his subordinates. [430] He was currently listening to Inspector Delmont Corrado's report. Corrado had officially taken charge of the case at Little Wraps, and Ridestad had summoned him back last night. Ridestad had high regard for Corrado, considering him to be a man of brains and imagination. What he appreciated even more was Corrado's strict self-discipline and steady demeanor, always double-checking every fact and keeping an open mind until the case was closed. [431] \"Inspector Legg took the call, sir,\" Corrado said. \"He seemed to handle it well, both decisively and wisely. It must have been a difficult situation, with about ten people all talking at once, including one from Central Europe. She was convinced that she would be locked up and was almost screaming to the point of collapsing the place.\" [432] \"The identity of the deceased has been confirmed?\" [433] \"Yes, sir. Rudolf Scherz, Swiss nationality. He worked as a receptionist at the Royal Spa Hotel in Medenham.\" [434] \"If you agree, sir, I will first go to the Royal Spa Hotel and then to Chipping Craighorn. Inspector Fletcher is already on the scene, and he will first see the people on the bus and then go to the mansion.\" [435] Ridestad nodded in agreement. The door opened, and the chief inspector looked up. [436] \"Come in, Henry,\" he said. \"We've had an unusual incident here.\" [437] Sir Henry Clersalin - former Commissioner of the Metropolitan Police - stepped into the room, frowning slightly. He was a tall, distinguished-looking man. [438] \"This may interest your tired palate,\" Ridestadle continued. [439] \"I've never been tired of it,\" Sir Henry grumbled. [440] \"The latest trick,\" Ridestadle said, \"is to advertise the murder before committing it. Show Henry the notice, Corridoke.\" [441] \"The 'Barnham News and Chipping Cleghorn Gazette',\" Sir Henry said. \"Splendid. He looked at the half-inch square of printed text pointed out by Cordeaux. [442] \"Ah, yes, quite unusual.\" [443] \"Any clues as to who placed the ad?\" Ridderdale asked. [444] \"According to the description, it was sent by Rudi Schlesinger himself - on Tuesday,\" Cordeaux replied. [445] \"No one questioned it? The recipient didn't find it odd?\" [446] \"The blonde girl who accepted the advertisement had a thyroid enlargement, I must say, sir, she wasn't very bright. She checked the word count and took the money.\" [447] \"What's this all about?\" asked Sir Henry. [448] \"It would pique the curiosity of many locals,\u201d Ridesdale speculated, \u201cto get them to a specific location at a specific time, and then detain them, searching them for cash and valuables. As an idea, it's not without its merits.\" [449] \"What's Chipping Craighorn like?\" asked Sir Henry. [450] \"A picturesque town, spread haphazardly. There's a butcher, a baker, a general store, and a rather nice antique shop, as well as two tea shops. It's a charming spot, suitable for both day trips and living. Former agricultural worker's cottages have been renovated and now house elderly spinsters and retired couples. Additionally, there are some Victorian-era buildings.\" [451] \"Well-bred old maids and retired majors,\" Sir Henry said, \"I see. Yes, if they saw that advertisement, they would all arrive at 6:30 to find out what's going on. Good heavens, if my particular old maid were here, she would certainly get involved, it would be right up her alley.\" [452] \"Who is your particular old maid, Henry, a cousin?\" [453] \"No,\" Sir Henry sighed, \"not a relative. She's just the best detective God ever created. A self-grown genius in the right soil.\" [454] He turned to Cordeaux. [455] \"Don't underestimate the old maid in this village, my child,\" he said, \"it could be a baffling case. I don't mean it has to be. But remember, the unmarried spinster who knits and gardens is far more astute than any police chief. She can tell you what might have happened, what should have happened, even what actually happened! And why it happened!\" [456] \"I will remember that, sir. \" Corrado Inspector answered very seriously, but no one would guess that Delmon Eric Corrado was actually Sir Henry's godson, and in a close, intimate relationship with his godfather. [457] Laidstall briefly gave his friend a summary of the case. [458] \"They all showed up at a quarter to six, that's a fact. \" He said. \"Did the Swissman know they would be there? And could they have brought a lot of cash and valuables to be stolen?\" [459] \"A couple of old-fashioned brooches, a few strands of small pearls \u2013 a little change, perhaps a couple of banknotes \u2013 that's all. \" Sir Henry mused. \"Does Miss Blacklock have a lot of money at home?\" [460] \"She said no, sir. Only five pounds in cash as far as I know. \" [461] \"Just chicken feed,\" Reddish said. [462] \"You mean,\" Sir Henry said, \"this guy likes to put on a show - not a robbery at all, just a prank, pretending to rob. Like in a movie? Well, quite possible. How did he shoot himself?\" [463] Reddish handed him a piece of paper. [464] \"According to the preliminary report from the forensic team, the revolver was fired at close range - the skin was burned. He... cannot prove whether it was an accident or suicide. It could have been intentional, or he could have tripped and fallen, and the revolver in his hand went off accidentally... I would say the latter.\" [465] \"They all saw something different,\" Detective Inspector Corradino said, disappointed. [466] \"This has always been interesting to me,\" said Henry Sir, \"what people really see in moments of extreme excitement and extreme nervousness. What they actually saw, and even more intr