n spoke:\n[490] \"Well, there were a couple of incidents, regarding the bills. There were items that shouldn't have been charged.\" [491] \"You mean you suspect him of charging certain fees that don't exist in the hotel's records and then pocketing the difference after the guests pay their bills?\u201d\n[492] \"Something like that... I guess he's just careless. There were a couple of instances where the amounts were quite significant. To be honest, I had the accountant check his books and suspected him... uh... of faking them. But despite all the errors, most of the accounts were reported fairly, and the actual cash balance was correct. So, I must have made a mistake myself.\" [493] \"What if you're not mistaken? If Sherz is skimming a little off the top in every place, wouldn't he use his own money to make up for the difference?\" [494] \"If he had that kind of money, then yes. But people who skim usually end up with very little money in their pockets.\" [495] \"So, if he needs money to make up for the difference, he would have to get it somehow \u2013 either by robbing or through some other means?\" [496] \"That's right. I was wondering if this was his first time doing it...\"\n[497] \"Maybe, it was pretty clumsy. Could he get money from anyone else? Does he have a woman in his life?\" [498] \"There's a waitress at the steakhouse named Mona Harris.\" [499] \"I should talk to her.\" [500] 3\n[501] Mona Harris is a pretty girl with bright red hair and a prominent nose. [502] She is cautious and alert, fearing that answering questions from the police could damage her reputation. [503] \"I don't know anything about this, sir. Not at all. If I had known that Rudy was like this, I would never have gone out with him. Of course, seeing him working at the front desk, I thought he was nice. I mean, the restaurant hires people \u2013 especially foreigners \u2013 they should be more careful. Because dealing with foreigners, you never know what they're like. I guess he's a member of one of the gangs you've listed?\" [504] \"We think,\" Corradino said, \"that he's working alone.\" [505] \"Strange \u2013 he was so quiet and respectable. Who would have thought? Although I have lost things \u2013 I remember now, a diamond brooch and a gold coin collection box. I'm sure it was. But I never would have thought it was Rudy.\" [506] \"I'm sure you wouldn't have thought so,\" said Colado. \"People get taken in. Are you close to him?\" [507] \"I don't know if I can say we're close.\" [508] \"But you liked each other?\" [509] \"Oh, we got along well \u2013 that's all. Not seriously, of course, I'm always very cautious with foreigners. They always have their own agendas. You can never tell who they are, can you? Some are Polish refugees from the war! Some are even Americans! They never mention they're married until it's too late. Rudi talks big \u2013 but I always take it with a grain of salt.\" [510] Colado seized on the word. [511] \"He talks big, does he? That's very interesting, Miss Harris. I can see that you'll be of great help to us. In what way does he exaggerate?\" [512] \"For example, how rich and prominent his family is in Switzerland\u2014how he can't bring the money here due to financial regulations. It's possible, I suppose. But his clothes aren't expensive. I mean, they're not up to par. And I remember, he used to tell me all these incredible stories about climbing the Alps and saving people's lives on glacier cliffs. But just walking the ridge of Mount B\u00fcrgerli made him dizzy. Pah, the Alps!\u201d\n[513] \"Do you spend a lot of time with him?\" [514] \"Yes\u2014uh\u2014yes. He's quite a gentleman, and he knows how to\u2014how to take care of a lady. He always chooses the best seats at the movies, and sometimes even buys me flowers. And his dancing is superb\u2014really superb.\" [515] \"Has he mentioned Miss Blacklock to you?\" [516] \"She sometimes comes here for lunch, doesn't she? She stayed here once. No, I don't think Rudi has ever mentioned her. I don't know if he knows her.\" [517] \"Has he mentioned Chipping Clegg-horn?\" [518] Mr. Corraduccio noticed a slight anxiety in Miss Harris's eyes, but he couldn't be sure. [519] \"I think not... I believe he did ask about the bus schedule once - about the timings - but I can't remember if it was going to Chipping Clegg-horn or somewhere else. That was some time ago.\" [520] From her, he couldn't get any more information. Rudi Scherz seemed ordinary. He hadn't seen him the night before last. He didn't know - not at all - he emphasized this - Rudi Scherz was a fraud. [521] Perhaps, thought Corradoch, that was the truth. [522] Chapter V: The Two Misses\n[523] 1\n[524] The small estate was exactly as Corradoch Inspector imagined. He noticed the ducks, the chickens, and a once-beautiful flowerbed with purple asters in their last bloom. The lawn and paths looked neglected. [525] \"All in all,\" Corradoch Inspector thought, \"this household probably doesn't have much money to hire a gardener, but they like flowers and have a knack for arranging them. The house needs a paint job, and most houses do these days. The small property is still in decent shape.\" [526] Corrado's car pulled up to the front door, and Chief Constable Fletcher emerged from the side of the house. He stood like a sentry, with a straight back and a military bearing, adept at conveying multiple meanings with a single word. [527] \"Sir.\" [528] \"Ah, Fletcher. There you are.\" [529] \"Sir.\" Chief Constable Fletcher replied. [530] \"Anything to report?\" [531] \"We've searched the whole house, sir. Sherris seems to have left no fingerprints anywhere. Of course, he was wearing gloves. There were no signs of forced entry through the doors or windows. He appears to have come by public bus from Medenham, arriving here at six o'clock. I found out that the side door was locked at five-thirty. It seems he must have come through the front door. Miss Blacklock stated that that door is usua