e, which killed poor Dora Bunner. And it couldn't have been Rudi Schlesinger, because he was dead as a doornail. It was the person in the living room on the night of the robbery, and that person may have been at the birthday party. The only one who wasn't there was Mrs. Harmon.\" [2297] \"You think someone put poisoned aspirins there on the night of the birthday party?\" [2298] \"Why not?\" [2299] \"But how could they do that?\" [2300] \"Well, we all went to the bathroom, didn't we?\" said Miss Inchcliff gruffly. \"I washed my hands because the cake was so sticky. And Miss Eastbrooke was powdering Miss Blacklock's face in the bathroom, wasn't she?\" [2301] \"Chin! You think it was her? \u2014\"\n[2302] \"I don't know. If she did, it would be too obvious. If you wanted to put the pills there, I don't think you'd want someone to see you in the bathroom, would you? There are plenty of other opportunities.\" [2303] \"The men didn't go upstairs.\" [2304] \"There's the back staircase. Besides, if a man left the house, you wouldn't follow him around to make sure he really went where you did, would you? That would be undignified! Anyway, don't argue with me, Miss Mulgrew. I want to start over from the first murder of Letty Blacklock. Now, first of all, remember the facts, because it all depends on you.\" [2305] Miss Mulgrew looked tense. [2306] \"Oh, dear, Chippy, you know I'm all mixed up about all this! -\"\n[2307] \"The problem is not with your brain, or with the gray cells you call your brain. The problem is with your eyes; the problem is what you saw at the time. -\"\n[2308] \"But I saw nothing. -\"\n[2309] \"As I said, your trouble is, Murgatroyd, you don't try hard enough. Now, here's what happened that night: whoever went to kill Letty Blacklock that night was in the house. He - I say he, because it's more convenient to say, but there's no reason to assume it was a man and not a woman, except that men are generally scoundrels - anyway, he oiled the door from the living room to the outside, which should have been nailed shut. Don't ask me when he did it, because that would muddy the waters. In fact, if I had to pick a time, I could walk into any pub in Chipping Clegg-horn and do anything I wanted in about half an hour without anyone noticing. Find out where the staff is, when the owner goes out, where exactly he's going, and for how long. Now, let's think, he oiled the second door so that it wouldn't make a sound when opened. The plan was: lights out, door A - the front door - opened with a bang. Flash a flashlight and say the word \"robbery\". Meanwhile, while we were all gaping, X - that's the best way to call him - slipped quietly from door B into the hallway, came up behind the Swiss idiot, and shot Letty Blacklock twice before shooting the Swiss. Then he dropped the gun. So only someone like you, who doesn't like to think, would think that was evidence of the Swiss firing the gun. Then he quickly made his way back to the living room while we were looking for a lighter. Understand? -\"\n[2310] \"Yes, yes, but who was it? -\"\n[2311] \"Well, if you don't know, Muggleton, then nobody does!\" [2312] \"Me?\" Muggleton exclaimed in surprise. \"I don't know. I really don't, Quinn!\" [2313] \"Use that thing you call a brain. First, where was everyone when the lights went out?\" [2314] \"I don't know.\" [2315] \"No, you do. You're just muddled, Muggleton. You know where you were yourself, don't you? You were behind the door.\" [2316] \"Yes, yes, I was behind the door. I bumped my toe when the door opened.\" [2317] \"Why can't you go see a proper foot doctor, instead of messing around with it? You're going to get gangrene one of these days. So, you were behind the door, I was standing by the fireplace, and I was just about to have a drink. Letty Blacklock was at the table in the arcade, reaching for a cigarette. Patrick went through the arcade to the small sitting room to get the drink Letty Blacklock had left there. Agreed?\" [2318] \"Yes, yes. I remember all that.\" [2319] \"Good. Now, someone was following Patrick through the small sitting room, or was about to go with him, a man. The question is, I can't remember whether it was Eastbrooke or Edmund Swetnam. Do you remember?\" [2320] \"No, I don't.\" [2321] \"You just can't remember! Someone else went to the small parlor, it was Philippa Hemmes. I remember this very well because I remember noticing how beautiful her straight back was, and I thought to myself: 'The girl would look good riding a horse.' I was looking at her, thinking about that. She walked to the fireplace in the small parlor, I don't know what she went there for, because just then the lights went out. [2322] \"That's how everyone was positioned: Patrick Simmons was in the living room, Philippa Hemmes was there, and either Eastbrook Colonel or Edmund Swetnam - but I don't know who. Now, Muggleton, pay attention, the most likely culprit is one of these three. Anyone who wanted to get out of the door from a distance would have to be in a convenient position to act as soon as the lights went out. So I say the most likely culprit is one of these three. If that's the case, Muggleton, you're out of luck!\" [2323] It was clear that Miss Muggleton's face lit up. [2324] \"On the other hand,\" Miss Chinchcliffe continued, \"it may not be any of the three. That's where you come in, Muggleton.\" [2325] \"But how could I know the situation at the time?\" [2326] \"I've already said that if you don't know, then no one does.\" [2327] \"But I don't know! I really don't know! I couldn't see anything!\" [2328] \"Oh, no, you could see. You were the only one who could see. You were standing behind the door, and you couldn't look at the flashlight because the door was between you and the flashlight. You were facing the other way, in the same direction as the flashlight. The rest of us were blinded by the flashlight, but you weren't.\" [2329] \"Yes, yes, maybe so, but I couldn't see anything, the flashlight was flashing around.\" [2330] \"What did it illuminate? Was it shining on everyone's faces? On the table? On the chairs?\" [2331] \"Yes, yes, that's right... Miss Bonner, she had her mouth wide open, her eyes bulging out, blinking in terror.\" [2332] \"That's right! It was hard to get you to use your gray cells, Miss Quincannon. So, what happened next?\" [2333] \"But I didn't see anything else, really.\" [2334] \"So, you saw an empty room? With no one standing or sitting?\" [2335] \"No, of course not. Miss Bonner had her eyes wide open, and Mrs. Harmon was sitting on the arm of a chair, with her eyes closed tightly and her hands covering her face \u2013 like a child.\" [2336] \"Very well, these are Mrs. Harmon and Miss Bonner. Do you still not understand what I'm getting at? The difficult part is that I don't want to put my thoughts into your head. But once we eliminate the people you have seen, we can get to the point, which is whether there are any people you haven't seen. Understood? Besides the table, chair, chrysanthemums, and so on, there are still some people left: Julia Simmons, Mrs. Swetnam, Mrs. Eastbrook \u2013 either Colonel Eastbrook or Mr. Edmund Swetnam, Miss Dora Bonner, and Round-harmon. Cross them off one by one. Now, think, Mr. Mulgrew, think carefully, are there any who were not present at the time?\" [2337] A branch hung onto the open window, and Miss Muggleton jumped slightly in fright. She closed her eyes and muttered to herself\u2026\n[2338] \"The flowers on the table... the big armchair... the flashlight's beam hasn't reached you yet, Mrs. Harmon, yes...\". [2339] The telephone rang urgently. Miss Quincy walked up to the telephone. [2340] \"Hello, yes. Police station?\" [2341] Miss Muggeridge closed her eyes tightly and relived the scene from the night of the 29th. Flashlight, slowly scanning... a group of people... the window... the sofa... Dora Bonner... the walls... the table with the lamp... the arcade... the revolver suddenly firing...\n[2342] \"...that's quite unusual!\" said Miss Muggeridge. [2343] \"What?\" Miss Chinchcliffe shouted into the phone, angry. \"Has it been there since this morning? When?\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nApparently, you don't\u2026well, there was Captain Eastbrook and his wife, Miss Chinchcliff and Miss Muggleton, Mrs. Sweetnam and Mr. Edmund Sweetnam, and Mrs. Hammond\u2014the vicar's wife. They all came in turn and said, 'I see you have the central heating on' and 'How lovely the chrysanthemums are!'\" [665] Colonel Corcoran bit his lip. She did a fair imitation. [666] \"Except for Mrs. Harmon, who was a bit thoughtless. She came in with her hat at a slant and her shoelaces untied, and asked outright when the murder was going to start. This put everyone in an awkward position, as they all pretended to be just dropping by. Aunt Letty said it should be starting soon. Then the clock struck, and just as the last note faded, the lights went out, and the door burst open, and a figure in a mask said something like 'Everyone, raise your hands.' It was just like an old movie, really quite ridiculous. Then he shot Aunt Letty twice, and suddenly it wasn't ridiculous anymore.\" [667] \"Where was everyone when all this happened?\" [668] \"When the lights went out? Well, everyone was just standing or sitting around, you can imagine. Mrs. Harmon was sitting on the sofa - Miss Chinchcliff, that's Miss Chinchcliff, was standing by the fireplace like a man.\" [669] \"Were you all in this room or the other one?\" [670] \"Mostly, I think, in this one. Patrick went to the other room to get sherry. I think Colonel Eastbrook went with him, but I'm not sure. We all - uh - just stood around, like I said.\" [671] \"Where were you?\" [672] \"I think I was standing by the window. Aunt Letty went to get cigarettes.\" [673] \"From the table by the arcade?\" [674] \"Yes - then the lights went out, and the old movie started.\" [675] \"What did the man with the flashlight do with it?\" [676] \"He shone it on us. It was so blinding, it made your eyes blink.\" [677] \"I need you to answer this question very carefully, Miss Simmons, was the flashlight in his hand stationary or moving?\" [678] Julia considered her response, her demeanor noticeably less irritating than before. [679] \"It was moving,\" she said slowly, \"like a disco ball. It was shining right in my eyes, then it moved around the room, and then there were two shots.\" [680] \"What happened next?\" [681] \"He turned around - and then, Mickey started screaming like a siren somewhere. His flashlight went out, and then there was a third shot. The door closed, slowly, you know, with this mournful sound, and it sounded terrible. We were all in the dark, not knowing what to do, poor Bonnie was squealing like a rabbit, and Mickey was screaming at the other end of the hallway.\" [682] \"Do you think the man shot himself on purpose or tripped and accidentally fired the revolver?\" [683] \"I have no idea. It felt so staged. I actually thought it was a joke - until I saw the blood on Letty's ear. But even if they wanted to make it look real, they should have shot a little higher up, right?\" [684] \"Indeed. Do you think he could see who he was shooting at, I mean, could Blacklock be clearly seen by the flashlight?\" [685] \"I don't know. I didn't look at her. I was looking at the man.\" [686] \"What I mean is, do you think the man intended to shoot at her - I mean, aimed specifically at her direction?\" [687] This idea seemed to surprise Julia. \"You mean, deliberately target Aunt Letty? Oh, I don't think so... after all, if he wanted to harm Aunt Letty secretly, there would be plenty of opportunities. There's no need to bring all the friends and neighbors together to make it more difficult. He could have done it on any day of the week, using the old and effective Irish way of hiding behind the fence and shooting her in the back, then making his escape.\" [688] And this - Corrigan thought, was a direct counter-argument to Dora Bunner's suggestion that the killer was deliberately attacking Letty Blacklock. [689] He sighed and said, \"Thank you, Miss Simmons. I should probably go see Micky now.\" [690] \"Beware of her nails,\" Julia warned, \"she's a Tartar!\" [691] 2\n[692] With Fletcher's guidance, Corradoch found Mickey in the kitchen. She was rolling out dough, and looked up at him suspiciously as he entered the room. [693] Her jet-black hair hung over her eyes, her face was somber, and her dark purple sweater and emerald green skirt contrasted sharply with her pale complexion. [694] \"What are you doing in my kitchen, police officer? You are the police, right? Always, always there is persecution \u2013 ah! Now I should be used to it. They say it's different in England, but no, it's the same. You're here to torture me, yes, to make me talk, but I won't say anything. You'll pull out my nails, burn my skin with matches \u2013 oh, worse than that. But I won't say anything, you hear? I won't say anything. You'll send me to a concentration camp, but I won't care.\" [695] Corradoch pondered her as he wondered which approach would be best to take. [696] Finally, he sighed, \"Well, then, put on your hat and coat.\" [697] \"What?\" Micky exclaimed in surprise. [698] \"Put on your hat and coat and come with me. I didn't bring any tools for torture or other such things; they're all at the station. Handcuffs, Fletcher?\" [699] \"Sir!\" Fletcher, the police chief, exclaimed in admiration. [700] \"But I don't want to go!\" Micky shrieked, shying away as she spoke. [701] \"Then you'll have to answer our polite questions like a civilized person. You can have a lawyer present if you like.\" [702] \"Lawyer? I don't like lawyers. I don't want a lawyer.\" [703] She put down the rolling pin, wiped her hands with a cloth, and sat down. [704] \"What do you want to know?\" she asked, frowning. [705] \"I want you to tell me what happened here last night.\" [706] \"You know what happened.\" [707] \"I want to hear your side of the story.\" [708] \"I was planning to leave. Did she tell you? I wanted to leave when I saw the ad about the murder in the paper. She wouldn't let me go. She was so heartless \u2013 no sympathy at all. She made me stay. But I knew \u2013 I knew something bad was going to happen. I knew I was going to be killed.\" [709] \"Well, you weren't murdered, were you?\" [710] \"Kind of,\" Mickey admitted reluctantly. [711] \"Now tell me what happened.\" [712] \"I was on edge. So tense, all night tense. I heard noises, sounds of someone moving about. I thought I heard someone moving around in the hall\u2014but it was only Mrs. Hyde passing through the hall from the side door. That way she wouldn't get the front steps dirty, she said. She's so careful! She's a Nazi herself, with that blonde hair and blue eyes, so arrogant, looking down her nose at me like I'm\u2014I'm just trash\u2014\"\n[713] \"Never mind Mrs. Hyde.\" [714] \"Who does she think she is? Did she go to an expensive university like me? Did she get an economics degree? No, she just works with her hands. She digs and cuts the grass, and she gets paid by the hour every Saturday. Who does she think she is, calling herself a lady?\" [715] \"I said, never mind Mrs. Hyde. Now go on.\" [716] \"I took the sherry, glasses, and little delicacies to the living room. Then the doorbell rang, so I went to answer it. It was bad form, but I did it. Then I went to the kitchen to polish the silver, because if someone came to kill me, I'd have a big knife right there, nice and sharp.\" [717] \"You're quite the planner.\" [718] \"Then, suddenly \u2014 I heard gunshots. I thought, 'Here it comes \u2014 it's started.' I ran through the dining room. The other door wouldn't open. I stopped to listen for a moment, then another shot, something heavy fell with a thud, just on the other side of the hall. I turned the doorknob, but the door was locked from the outside. I was locked in, like a mouse in a trap. I was so scared I went crazy, I screamed and pounded on the door. Finally \u2014 finally \u2014 they turned the key and let me out. Then I got candles \u2014 lots of candles \u2014 and then the lights came on, and I saw blood \u2014 blood! Oh, God, human blood! I've seen blood before. I saw my little brother \u2014 I saw him killed right in front of me \u2014 I've seen street blood \u2014 people shot dead \u2014 I \u2014\"\n[719] \"Yes,\" said Inspector Krogh, \"thank you very much.\" [720] \"Now,\" said Mickey in a dramatic tone, \"you can take me to the jailhouse!\" [721] \"There's still time,\" Inspector Corrado said. [722] 3\n[723] Inspector Corrado and Fletcher walked through the hall to the front door when the front door was pushed open, and a handsome young man almost bumped into them. [724] \"Be careful, now,\" the young man said. [725] \"Mr. Patrick Simmons?\" [726] \"That's me, Inspector. You're the inspector, right, and the other one is the sheriff?\" [727] \"Indeed, Mr. Simmons. Can I speak with you?\" [728] \"I'm innocent, Inspector. I swear I'm innocent.\" [729] \"Cut the crap, Mr. Simmons. I have a lot of people to see and I don't want to waste time. What's this room for? Can we go in?\" [730] \"It's what they call a study\u2014but no one reads.\" [731] \"You are said to be studying?\" asked Coradino. [732] \"I found myself unable to concentrate on mathematics, so I came back.\" [733] Coradino asked for full name, age, and details of his service during the war. [734] \"Now, Mr. Simmons, can you describe what happened last night?\" [735] \"We had a dinner party. That is, Micky prepared delicious pastries, Aunt Letty opened a bottle of sherry \u2013\"\n[736] Coradine interrupted him:\n[737] \"A new bottle? And another one that had been opened?\" [738] \"Yes, half full. But Aunt Letty didn't seem to like it.\" [739] \"Was she nervous?\" [740] \"Not really, she's quite astute. I think it was old Bonnie who made her nervous\u2014constantly predicting disasters.\" [741] \"So, Miss Bonner was quite worried?\" [742] \"Yes, she was. She was very serious about it.\" [743] \"Did she take the ad seriously?\" [744] \"She was terrified.\" [745] \"Miss Blacklock seemed to think it was about you when she first saw the ad. What was that all about?\" [746] \"Of course, everything here can be blamed on me!\" [747] \"Are you involved in this, Mr. Simmons?\" [748] \"Me? Not at all.\" [749] \"Have you ever met or spoken to Rudy Schlesinger?\" [750] \"I've never met him in my life.\" [751] \"But that's the kind of joke you would play?\" [752] \"Who told you that? Just because I once got apple pie on Bonnie's bed and sent a postcard to Mickey saying the Gestapo was on her trail\u2014\"\n[753] \"Stop talking about other things and tell me what you think about yesterday's incident.\" [754] \"I was just walking into the small living room to get some wine when suddenly the lights went out. I turned around and there was a guy at the door saying 'Hands up' and then everyone started gasping and screaming. I was thinking\u2014can I make a sudden move on him? And then he fired his gun and he fell to the ground and the flashlight went out and we were back in the dark. Then Colonel Eastbrook gave an order in his military voice. 'Bring some light.' He asked if my lighter could be lit? No, it wouldn't light, those damn new inventions are all like that.\" [755] \"Do you think the intruder was aiming at Miss Blacklock?\" [756] \"How should I know? Maybe he just took out his revolver for fun\u2014then maybe went too far.\" [757] \"And then he shot himself?\" [758] \"Maybe. When I saw his face, I noticed he looked pale, like a jittery little thief.\" [759] \"You're sure you've never seen him before?\" [760] \"Never.\" [761] \"Thank you, Mr. Simmons. I'm going to speak to the others who were there last night. Who should I start with?\" [762] \"Well, our Philly\u2014Mrs. Haymes works at Darcy's Manor. The gate to that place is almost directly across from mine. And the Sweatnam's family has been around here lately. Anyone will point you in the right direction.\" [763] Chapter 7: The Gathering\n[764] 1\n[765] Darcy's Manor must have seen its fair share of hardship in the years of war. The wild grasses flourished in the former vegetable garden, with only a few stalks of asparagus left swaying in the breeze as a testament to the past. Thistles, bindweed, and other invasive weeds thrived and grew vigorously. [766] It is clear that part of the garden has been converted into a training ground. Here, Kolarczyk finds an elderly man with a furrowed brow leaning on a shovel, lost in thought. [767] \"Are you looking for Mrs. Hems? I'm not sure where you'll find her. She has her mind set on what she wants to do, and she won't listen to anyone else. I could show her how to do it - if she wanted to - but what's the point? These young ladies think they know everything just because they put on pants and drive a tractor around the field. But what we need here is tending to the garden. That's not something you learn overnight. Gardening, that's what we need here.\" [768] \"It seems that way,\" Kolarczyk said. [769] The old man took this as an insult. [770] \"Take a good look, sir. Do you think I can do anything about this big plot of land? Three men and a boy, that's all we had before. Now we need that many. But not many men can work like me. Sometimes I have to work until eight in the evening. Eight at night.\" [771] \"What do you use to light your way when you work at night? A lantern?\" [772] \"I mean, of course, the summer nights. Of course, I mean the summer nights.\" [773] \"Oh,\" Kradok replied, \"I'll go find Mrs. Haims.\" [774] The countryman showed some interest. [775] \"What do you need her for? You're a policeman, right? Does she have some trouble? Or is it something to do with the estate? Thieves broke in, waving revolvers and threatening everyone. Such things didn't happen before the war. Desperadoes roaming around the countryside. Why doesn't the army round them all up?\" [776] \"I don't know,\" Corradino said, \"but this robbery has stirred up a lot of talk.\" [777] \"Well, of course it has. What could have caused this? Ned Barker says it's because people watch too many movies. But Tom Liley says it's because we let the foreigners run around. He's sure, he says, that the girl who cooks for Miss Blacklock is to blame - she's a communist, or worse, he says, but we don't like that kind of thing here. And then there's Maryann, the one at the pub, you know, who says Miss Blacklock must have valuable things. 'You wouldn't believe it,' she says, 'because I'm sure Miss Blacklock is always on the lookout, but unfortunately her string of pearls is fake.' But then she said - maybe they were real. But Flore (Bella's daughter) said, 'Nonsense,' she said, 'it's just costume jewelry.' She said that. Costume jewelry - putting on a string of fake pearls is a good way to get by. The country gentry used to call it Roman pearls or Paris diamonds - I know because my wife used to be a lady's maid, and I know this.\" [778] Old Ash paused for breath, then continued:\n[779] \"I know that Miss Blacklock doesn't have much money,\" Jim Hawkins said. \"My wife works at the ranch, and she knows all about it.\" [780] \"Did he say what Mrs. Hawkins thought about the whole thing?\" [781] \"He said that Micky was involved in it, that's what she said. Micky's got a temper and a swagger about her! One morning she even called her a maid to her face. [782] Corradino stood for a moment, going over in his mind what the old gardener had said, trying to make sense of it all. He had a clear overall impression of the villagers of Chipping Clerghorn, but he felt it didn't help much with the case. He turned away, the old man calling out behind him, \"You might find her in the apple orchard. She's so young, she'd be better suited to picking apples than I am.\" [783] And it was in the orchard that Corradino found Phyllis Hammond. The inspector saw a pair of pretty legs climb down the trunk of a tree, followed by a bottom. Then Phyllis stood there with her hair tousled by the branches, her cheeks flushed, looking at him in surprise. [784] \"What a Rosalind,\" Corradino thought to himself. He was a big fan of Shakespeare and had played the melancholy Jaques in the Police Orphanage's production of As You Like It. [785] A moment later he corrected his opinion. Phyllis Hammond was too wooden, her beauty and passive nature had a distinctly British style, but of the twentieth century, not the sixteenth. She was well-bred, restrained, and lacked the spark of wit. [786] \"Good morning, Mrs. Hammers. I'm terribly sorry to startle you. I'm Inspector Corradoch from the Middlesex Police. I'd like to speak to you.\" [787] \"About last night?\" [788] \"Yes.\" [789] \"For a long time?\" [790] She looked around suspiciously. [791] Colado gestured to a fallen tree trunk.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[936] 6\n[937] Inspector Corridor looked around the shabby house with a touch of satisfaction. It reminded him of his home in Cumberland. The faded polished prints, the worn armchairs, the scattered flowers and books, the long-haired droopy-eared dog in the basket. And Mrs. Harmon's agitated expression, her disheveled appearance, and her eager face, elicited his sympathy and a sense of familiarity. [938] But she came straight to the point: \"I'm afraid I can't help you. I closed my eyes. I hate being dizzy. Then the gun went off, and I closed my eyes even tighter. I wish, oh, I wish the murder had been silent. I don't like gunshots.\" [939] \"So you saw nothing,\" said the inspector with a smile. \"But you heard, didn't you?\" [940] \"Ah, my goodness, yes, there was quite a bit to hear. Opening and closing doors, people saying silly things, gasping, and, oh, old Micky screaming like a steam whistle \u2013 and poor Bonnie squealing like a rabbit caught in a trap. Everyone was pushing and shoving, falling over each other. But when the gunshots stopped, I opened my eyes. Then everyone was holding candles in the hallway. The lights came on, and suddenly everything was back to normal \u2013 not exactly the same as before, but everyone was calm again, not \u2013 trapped in the dark. People in the dark are different, aren't they?\" [941] \"I think I understand what you mean, Mrs. Harmon.\" [942] Mrs. Harmon smiled at him. [943] \"He was there,\" she said. \"A sneaky-looking foreigner - pink-faced, surprised expression - lying on the ground, dead - with a revolver by his side. It was - oh, well, it seemed there was no logic to it.\" [944] Inspector Kodakov couldn't make sense of it either. The whole incident troubled him. [945] * * *\n[946] [1] Rosalind, the female lead in Shakespeare's play \"As You Like It.\" [947] [2] A precursor to the jack-o'-lantern, carved out of a turnip, to represent a cursed ghost. [948] Chapter 8: The Detective Appears\n[949] 1\n[950] Coleridge placed all the interview records on the desk in front of the Chief, who had just finished reading a telegram from Swiss police. [951] \"He has a criminal record,\" said Ridestad, \"Well \u2013 no surprise there.\" [952] \"Yes, sir.\" [953] \"Jewelry... yes... forged documents for entry... oh... checks... a real con artist.\" [954] \"Yes, sir \u2013 in the small things.\" [955] \"Indeed. Small evils can lead to great disasters.\" [956] \"I disagree, sir.\" [957] The director looked up. [958] \"Are you worried, Corrado?\" [959] \"Yes, sir.\" [960] \"What is it? This is a clear-cut case, isn't it? Let's see what these people you questioned have to say.\" [961] He moved the report in front of him and quickly went through it. [962] \"Common sense - inconsistencies and contradictions. Different people's accounts of tense moments are bound to be different. But the overall picture seems clear.\" [963] \"I know, sir - but this picture doesn't sit right with me. If you understand what I mean - it doesn't feel right.\" [964] \"Well, let's stick to the facts. Rudi Scherz took the 5:20 bus from Medenham to Chipping Cleghorne at 6:00, with a ticket and two witnesses. He walked in the direction of the small field after getting off the bus. He didn't put up much of a fight - probably from the front door - and got into the house. He fired a revolver at the people inside, hitting Miss Blacklock lightly and then killing himself. Whether it was an accident or suicide, there's not enough evidence. I agree that the reason for doing it is not convincing. But that's not the question we should be answering. The coroner's verdict is: possibly suicide - possibly an accident. Whatever the outcome, it's the same for us. We can write up the case report now.\" [965] \"So, you mean we can always rely on Colonel Eastbrook's psychological theories?\" Corradino said dejectedly. [966] Ridestad laughed. [967] \"After all, Colonel Eastbrook may be experienced,\" he said. \"I dislike how people nowadays always throw around psychological terms - but we can't rule out that factor either.\" [968] \"I still feel that the whole incident is not right, sir.\" [969] \"Is there any reason to believe that someone in Chipping Creagour lied to you?\" [970] Corradoc hesitated. [971] \"I think the foreign girl knows more than she's letting on. But that could be my prejudice.\" [972] \"Do you think she might have conspired with this man? Let him in? Encourage him to commit the crime?\" [973] \"Something like that. I think she might have. But that would mean there's something valuable in the house, money or jewels or something. But it doesn't seem to be the case. Miss Blacklock is adamant there's nothing valuable, and so are the others. All this can only lead us to assume there's something valuable in the house, but no one else knows -\"\n[974] \"It sounds like a plot from a bestseller.\" [975] \"I agree it sounds absurd, sir. Moreover, Miss Bonner is convinced that Shurz attempted to murder Miss Blacklock.\" [976] \"So, from what you've told me\u2014from her testimony\u2014Miss Bonner\u2014\"\n[977] \"Ah, I agree, Inspector,\" Corradoch quickly interjected, \"she's an absolutely unreliable witness, easily influenced by suggestions. Anyone could put something in her head\u2014but interestingly, this idea is entirely her own theory\u2014no one has suggested anything to her. Everyone denies it. She's finally broken from the pack. What she says is based solely on her impressions.\" [978] \"Why would Rudy Sherlock kill Miss Blacklock?\" [979] \"That's the problem, Inspector. I don't know. Miss Blacklock doesn't know\u2014unless she's a better liar than I think she is. Nobody knows. So it's probably not true.\" [980] He sighed. [981] \"Cheer up, Corradoch,\" the commissioner said, \"I'll take you out, and we'll have lunch with Sir Henry at the Meddenham Royal Spa Hotel. We'll try their best dishes.\" [982] \"Thank you, sir,\" Corradoch said, somewhat surprised. [983] \"You see, we received a letter -\" The commissioner suddenly cut off his words as Sir Henry Clithering entered the room, \"Ah, you're here, Henry.\" [984] \"Good morning, Delmon,\" Sir Henry said, this time in a more casual tone. [985] \"I have something for you, Henry,\" the commissioner said. [986] \"What is it?\" [987] \"A personal letter from an old maid. She lives at the Royal Spa Hotel. It's some information she thinks may be useful to us regarding the case at Chipping Cleghorn.\" [988] \"An old maid,\" said Sir Henry, smugly, \"What did I tell you? They hear everything, see everything. But they don't say what they hear and see, do they? What's this special talent of hers?\" [989] Laidlaw looked at the letter. [990] \"Just like my grandmother wrote,\" he complained, \"Full of sharp points. Like spiders in the inkwell, all underlined. Started off by saying she hopes it won't take up too much of our valuable time, but may be of some slight assistance, etc., etc. What's her name? Jane - what - Mopple - no, Mapple, Jane Mapple.\" [991] \"My God,\" said Sir Henry. \"How convenient. George, that's exactly the special talent, the one and only, four-star old maid. The super-old maid. She managed to get to Meddingham instead of sitting quietly at home in St. Mary Mead, just in time to stir up a murder case. Another murder to advertise - just to amuse Miss Mapple.\" [992] \"Well, Henry,\" said Ridderdale, with a touch of mockery. \"I'm glad to meet your perfect lady. Come on! Let's go to the Pavilion and meet this lady. Look, Colonel Crook looks suspicious.\" [993] \"No, sir,\" answered Crook politely. But he was wondering if his godfather wasn't a bit too fond of exaggeration at times. [994] 2\n[995] Miss Jane Mapple, even if not quite as close to Crook's imagination as he had thought, was not far off. She was far kinder and older than he had imagined. She looked truly weather-beaten. Her hair was white, her pink face was deeply wrinkled, her blue eyes were soft and innocent, and she was wrapped in a thick woolen sweater. She had a woolen lace shawl draped over her shoulders and was busy knitting a baby blanket. [996] When she saw Sir Henry, she was so excited that she couldn't speak coherently, and when introduced to the Inspector and Inspector Corrigan, she was even more excited. [997] \"Really, Sir Henry, it's such an honor... Such an honor. It's been so long since I saw you... Yes, my rheumatism has been terrible lately. Of course, I couldn't afford to stay at this hotel, but their prices are insane. But Raymond\u2014my nephew Raymond West, you may remember him\u2014\"\n[998] \"Everyone knows him.\" [999] \"Yes. This charming boy's books have always been successful\u2014he's proud of never writing anything cheerful. This charming boy insists on paying for all my expenses. And his charming wife, the artist, has made a name for herself. Mainly with her dead flowers in pots and broken combs. I never dared tell her, but I still admire Blair Leithon and Alma Tadema. Oh, look, I'm rambling again. And the Police Commissioner himself\u2014I never expected\u2014I'm so afraid of taking up his time\u2014\"\n[1000] \"A real old fool,\" Inspector Corrigan grumbled to himself, feeling annoyed. [1001] \"Go to the manager's private office,\" Laidlaw said. \"We can talk there.\" [1002] So, Miss Marple took off her woolen shawl and gathered her spare knitting needles, and then she joined them in the comfortable lounge of Mr. Rolleston, grumbling all the way. [1003] \"Well, Miss Marple, let's hear what you have to say,\" the Inspector said. [1004] Miss Marple introduced her point in a surprisingly succinct manner. [1005] \"It was a check,\" she said. \"He forged the check.\" [1006] \"He?\" [1007] \"The young man who works at the counter here, the one who's supposed to have directed that robbery and shot himself.\" [1008] \"You mean he forged a check?\" [1009] Miss Maple nodded. [1010] \"Yes. I brought it.\u201d She pulled the check out of her bag and placed it on the table. \"Here, it came with the rest of my things this morning from the bank. You see, it was seven pounds, and he changed it to seventeen. He added a line in front of the seven, and then added a ten after the seven [3], and cleverly blurred the whole thing with a little smudge of ink. It was very well done. I think he's a professional. What do you think?\" [1011] \"He picked the wrong person this time,\" said Sir Henry. [1012] Miss Marple nodded in agreement. [1013] \"Yes, he shouldn't have gone too far down the criminal path. It was a mistake for him to target me. Busy young married women or love-struck girls \u2013 they don't care about the numbers on the check and won't check their account balances. But an old lady who's used to being thrifty \u2013 that's the wrong target. Seventeen pounds is a number I wouldn't sign. Twenty pounds is a fixed monthly expense. As for my personal expenses, I usually exchange seven pounds in cash \u2013 it used to be five pounds, but everything has gone up now.\" [1014] \"Perhaps he reminded you of someone?\" Sir Henry asked, with a sly look in his eyes. [1015] Miss Marple smiled and shook her head. [1016] \"You're a cheeky one, Sir Henry. Yes, he did. Fred Taylor, the fishmonger. He always adds an extra shilling to the pounds column. With everyone eating so much fish now, the bill gets longer, and many people don't add up the numbers themselves. There's always an extra ten shillings in his pocket, which is enough for him to buy a few ties and take Jesse Spragg - the girl from the haberdashery - to the movies. That's what these young fellows want to do. Well, when I first came here, my bill was off. I pointed it out to the boy, and he was very sincere in his apology and looked very guilty. But at the time, I thought to myself, 'You have a very deceptive gaze, young man,' and I meant the kind of stare that fixes you with its gaze.\" [1017] Colonel Corcoran suddenly felt a surge of admiration. \"Just like the real Jimmie Kelly,\" he thought, recalling the notorious con artist he had recently helped capture. [1018] \"Rudy Schlesinger is a glutton,\" said Ridley. \"We found he had a criminal record in Switzerland.\" [1019] \"I guess he couldn't stay there and then came here with a fake ID?\" Miss Marple asked. [1020] \"That's right,\" Ridley answered. [1021] \"He often goes out with the red-haired waitress in the dining room,\" Miss Marple said. \"Fortunately, I don't think she's interested. She just likes someone who's a bit 'different.' He buys her flowers and chocolates, and British boys don't usually do that. Did she tell you everything she knows?\u201d She suddenly turned to Kolarov. \"Or did she hold back?\" [1022] \"I'm not entirely sure,\" Kolarov said cautiously. [1023] \"I think she's still holding something back,\" Miss Marple said. \"She looks worried. This morning, she sent me salted fish instead of herring, and she forgot to bring the milk jug. She's usually an excellent waitress. Yes, she's worried, afraid she might have to testify. But I hope\u2014\" Her blue eyes looked straight and frank, with a purely female, Victorian admiration for the handsome and masculine Kolarov Inspector, \"you can convince her to tell you everything she knows.\" [1024] Kolarov Inspector blushed, and Sir Henry smiled to himself. [1025] \"This could be important,\" Miss Marple said. \"He might have told her who he was.\" [1026] Ridderdale stared at her in amazement. [1027] \"Who?\" he asked. [1028] \"I didn't express myself clearly. What I meant was who made him do it.\" [1029] \"So you think someone else made him do it?\" [1030] Miss Marple looked astonished. [1031] \"Oh, it's only natural, isn't it? I mean... a handsome young man like that... he'd pick up a little here, a little there... alter a small check, perhaps steal a few trinkets left behind by someone, or even take a bit of money from the cash register... all little things, really, just to have some ready cash so he could dress well and take a girl out, and so on. But suddenly, he went crazy, took out a revolver, and held everyone in the room hostage, even firing at people. He could never have done that - not at any time! He's not that kind of person. It doesn't make sense.\" [1032] Corcoran took a sharp breath. Lady Dedlock had said it. The vicar's wife had said it. And his own feelings were growing stronger. It doesn't make sense. Now the old maid was saying it, too, in her slow, definite manner. [1033] \"Perhaps you could tell us, Miss Marple,\" he said, suddenly becoming very forceful, \"what happened?\" [1034] She turned to him in surprise. [1035] \"But how would I know what happened? There's a report, of course, but it's very brief. I can make some guesses, but I lack concrete evidence.\" [1036] \"George,\" said Sir Henry, \"would it be out of order for Miss Maple to see the transcript of Coradine's conversation with the people in Chipping Cleghorn? Would that be a breach of protocol?\" [1037] \"It might be,\" said Ridesdale, \"but I got to where I am by not being overly concerned with rules. She can look. I'd like to hear her opinion.\" [1038] Miss Maple felt very embarrassed. [1039] \"I'm afraid you've always been too ready to take Henry's word for it. Henry has always been kind. He overvalues any small observations I've made. In fact, I have no special talents\u2014none at all\u2014just a little knowledge of human nature. I find people too trusting of others. And I fear I always believe the worst. It's not a good quality, but it's often proved right by a series of events.\" [1040] \"Here you are,\" said Ridesdale, handing her a stack of typing paper. \"It won't take you long. After all, these people are like you\u2014you've seen many of them. You may find something we've missed. The case is about to be closed, and we'd like to hear what an amateur detective has to say. I can tell you, Coradine is not satisfied. He said it doesn't make sense, just like you.\" [1041] Miss Maple read the report in silence. Finally, she put down the typing paper. [1042] \"Very interesting,\" she sighed. \"Everyone has their own opinion. What they saw\u2014or thought they saw. Everything is so complicated, almost all trivial details, and it's hard to see any important clues\u2014like finding a needle in a haystack.\" [1043] Corrigan was disappointed. For a moment, he had thought that Henry's recommendation of the famous Miss Maple might be right. She might hit on something\u2014old people's feelings are often very sharp. For instance, he couldn't hide anything from his Aunt Emma. Later, she told him that whenever he was about to lie, his nose would twitch. [1044] However, the famous Miss Maple could only come up with some silly generalizations. He was annoyed with her, so he said rather rudely, \"The point is, the facts are indisputable. No matter how contradictory the details these people provide, they saw the same thing. They saw a masked man with a revolver and a flashlight, holding them up. Whether they thought he said 'put up your hands' or 'money or your life' or some other jargon related to their idea of a hold-up, they saw him.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHammers?\" [2114] \"I mean, what are you going to do? Tell everyone? Is that necessary? Fair? Conscience clear?\" [2115] \"Does no one know about this?\" [2116] \"No one here knows,\" her voice changed, \"my son, he doesn't know. I don't want him to know. I'll never let him know.\" [2117] \"So I have to say, Mrs. Hemes, you're taking a big risk. Tell him when he grows up and understands. But if one day he discovers the truth on his own, it won't be good for him. If you continue to tell him that his father is a heroic martyr\u2014\"\n[2118] \"I don't do that. I'm not completely dishonest, I just don't mention it. His father is dead. After all, that's all we know.\" [2119] \"But your husband is still alive?\" [2120] \"Maybe, how would I know?\" [2121] \"When was the last time you saw him, Mrs. Hammers?\" [2122] Phyllida answered quickly: \"I haven't seen him for years.\" [2123] \"You're sure about that? For example, you didn't see him two weeks ago?\" [2124] \"What are you implying?\" [2125] \"That you met with Rudi Scherz in the gazebo, I never thought that was likely. But Micky's story was so convincing. I believe, Mrs. Hammers, that the man you saw when you came back from work that morning was your husband.\" [2126] \"I didn't meet anyone in the gazebo.\" [2127] \"Maybe he was short of money and you helped him out?\" [2128] \"I told you I haven't seen him. I didn't meet anyone in the gazebo!\" [2129] \"Deserters are usually desperate men. You know, they often get involved in robberies, burglaries, and the like. And they bring back foreign-made revolvers from abroad.\" [2130] \"I don't know where my husband is. I haven't seen him for many years.\" [2131] \"Is that your final statement, Mrs. Hammers?\" [2132] \"I have nothing else to say.\" [2133] \"This is your final statement, Mrs. Hammers?\" [2134] \"I have nothing else to say.\" [2135] \"Stubborn as a mule.\" He muttered to himself, angry. He was sure that Mrs. Hammers was lying, but he couldn't break her stubborn denial. [2136] He wished he knew more about this former Captain Himmelman. The information he had was scant, with a tarnished military record, but that didn't mean Himmelman had fallen into criminality. [2136]\n[2137] After all, Himmelman had nothing to do with the oil on the door. [2137]\n[2138] It was someone in the house, or someone who could easily enter the house. [2138]\n[2139] He stood looking up the stairs, suddenly wondering what Julia was doing up in the attic. An attic, he mused, was not a place where the fastidious Julia would willingly venture. [2139]\n[2140] What was she doing up there? [2140]\n[2141] He creeps up the stairs, no one around, and pushes open the door that Julia had come out of, climbing the narrow staircase up to the attic. [2142] There are some large trunks, small trunks, and various broken household items, such as a chair missing a leg, a broken ceramic table lamp, and some antique cutlery. [2143] He turns to the large trunk and opens one of the lids. It's all clothes. Old-fashioned, of good quality, all women's wear. He assumes they belong to Miss Blacklock or her deceased sister. [2144] He opens another trunk. [2145] It's all curtains. [2146] He turned to a small briefcase, which contained some documents and letters. The letters were old and the paper was