od at the bottom of the staircase, staring at him in astonishment. \"Was it you up there? I heard footsteps, and I couldn't imagine who \u2013\"\n[2160] \"Miss Blacklock, I found some letters you wrote to your sister many years ago. May I take them back to look at them?\" [2161] She flushed with anger. [2162] \"Must you do this? What good will it do you?\" [2163] \"They might give me a picture of Sonia Goddard, her character \u2013 maybe there will be some clues that could help with the case \u2013 and the events.\" [2164] \"These are private letters, Inspector.\" [2165] \"I know.\" [2166] \"I guess you'll take them... I suppose you have the right to, anyway you could easily get hold of them. Take them \u2013 take them! But you won't find much about Sonia in them. She married and left me when I started working for Randall Goodale a year or two later.\" [2167] \"There might be something,\" Corrado insisted. \"We can't leave any stone unturned. I assure you, there is a danger to you.\" [2168] She bit her lip. \"I understand. Bonnie died \u2013 from aspirin meant for me. Next could be Patrick, or Julia, Philly, or Micky \u2013 any of the promising young people. Or the person who gave me the drink, or the one who gave me the chocolate. Oh! Take the letters \u2013 take them. Burn them after you've read them. They mean nothing to me and Charlotte now. The past is over \u2013 gone \u2013 done with. No one remembers \u2013\"\n[2169] She lifted her hand and touched the false pearl necklace she wore. Corrado thought it clashed with her tweed suit and skirt. [2170] She repeated, \"Take the letters.\" [2171] 3\n[2172] The next afternoon, the Inspector visited the parsonage. [2173] It was a dark, windy day. Miss Marple pulled her chair closer to the fire and knitted wool. Roundworm crawled around the floor, cutting fabric according to the pattern. [2174] Miss Marple leaned back and brushed a strand of hair out of her eyes, looking expectantly at Coradine. [2175] \"I don't know if this violates confidentiality,\" the Inspector said to Miss Marple, \"but I'd like you to see this letter.\" [2176]He explained the circumstances under which he had found these letters in the attic. [2177]\"They were quite moving letters,\" he said, \"written by Miss Blacklock to keep her sister interested in life, to keep her in good health. Behind these sisters, there was a very clear image of their old-fashioned father, Dr. Blacklock. A true stick-in-the-mud, a bully, utterly self-righteous, and convinced that everything he thought and did was right. Perhaps because of his stubbornness, he had killed hundreds of patients. He could not tolerate any new ideas or methods.\" [2178]\"I don't know if I should blame him for that,\" Miss Marple said. \"I always thought that young doctors were eager to try and rush things. After they had extracted all our teeth, filled our strange glands with drugs, and gradually removed our organs, they admitted that they were powerless. Honestly, I prefer the old-fashioned remedies, the ones in the black bottles. After all, you can just pour the medicine down the drain.\" [2179]She took the letter from Colonel Darcy. [2180]He spoke up: \"I asked you to look at this letter because I thought you would understand this generation better than I do. I really don't understand what's going on in their heads.\" [2181] Miss Marple opened the brittle paper. [2182] My dearest Charlotte:\n[2183] I haven't written to you for two days because we've had the most terrible family quarrel. Sonya, Randell's sister (do you remember her? She picked you up in her car that day. I wish you would go out more often.) Sonya announced that she was going to marry a man named Dmitri Stanforth. I've only met him once. He's very attractive, but I must say, he's not to be relied upon. Randell strongly opposed him, calling him a scoundrel and a fraud. Bella, may the Lord bless her, just smiled and lay on the couch. Sonya, who had been expressionless, flew into a rage, ready to kill Randell. Yesterday, I thought she would! [2184] I've done my best. I talked to Sonya and to Randell, trying to get them to think rationally. When they got together, they started arguing again: you can't imagine how boring it was. Randell has been trying to find out more about Stanforth, and it seems that he's no good at all. [2185] Meanwhile, the business has been neglected. I've been working in the office, and in a way, it's been quite interesting because Randell has let me do it. Yesterday, he said to me, \"Thank God, there's at least one sane person in the world. You wouldn't fall in love with a scoundrel, would you, Blatch?\" I said I didn't think I would. Randell said, \"Let's discuss some of the minor issues in London.\" He can be a mischievous devil at times, and he can be hasty in a crisis. [2186] \"Are you determined to be an honest person, Blatch?\" he said one day. And I was indeed intending to be so! I cannot understand how people can ignore deceit, but Randall is really not clear-sighted. He only knows what is truly illegal. [2187] Bella just laughs at all this. She thinks that the whole Sonya affair is nonsense. \"Sonya has money of her own,\" she said, \"Why shouldn't she marry the man if she wants to?\" I said that this marriage would be a terrible mistake, and Bella said, \"Marriage to a man you love can never be a mistake \u2013 even if you regret it later.\" She also said, \"I think Sonya doesn't want to quarrel with Randall for the money. She really likes money.\" [2188] That's the situation now. How is your father? I will not say \"with regards\" to him. But if you think it's good, you can say it. Have you met more people lately? Dear, you cannot always be so sickly. Sonya sent her regards. She just came in, clenching and unclenching her hands, like an angry cat sharpening its claws. I think she had another argument with Randall. Of course, Sonya is good at stirring up trouble. She always stares at you with a calm gaze that makes you dare not look back at her. [2189] Your sister deeply loves you, dear, and you must pull yourself together. This iodine therapy will be different. I have been consulting with others, and it seems that iodine therapy is indeed very effective. [2190] Yours sincerely, Letitia\n[2191] Miss Maple folded the letter and handed it back to the inspector. Her expression was somewhat absent-minded. [2192] \"What do you think of her?\" Colonel Corder urged. \"What impression do you get of her?\" [2193] \"About Sonya? It's difficult to see one person through the eyes of another... She has decided to take her share - I think that's for sure. And she wants to be on top in both worlds...\"\n[2194] \" 'As if an angry cat, clenching and unclenching its hands,' Colonel Corder muttered. \"You know, that phrase reminds me of someone...\"\n[2195] He frowned. [2196] \"Consultations...\" Miss Marple murmured. [2197] \"I wish we could get the results of those consultations,\" Cora said. [2198] \"Does this letter remind you of anything at St. Mary Mead?\" Round asked, but her words were muffled by the safety pin in her mouth. [2199] \"I'm not sure, dear... Dr. Blacklock might be a bit like the missionary Mr. Curtis in Wychwood-on-Lea. He wouldn't let his children wear braces. He said if their teeth grew crooked, that was God's will. 'After all,' I told him, 'you shave and get your hair cut. Letting your hair grow long might be God's will too.' He said that was different. Classic machismo. But it doesn't help us with our current problem.\" [2200] \"We haven't been able to trace the revolver. It wasn't Rudi Schlesinger's. If we could find out who in Chipping Cleghorn had a revolver...\"\n[2201] \"Captain Eastbrook has one,\" Round said. \"It's in the drawer where he keeps his collars.\" [2202] \"How do you know that, Mrs. Harmon?\" [2203] \"Mrs. Bartlett told me, she's my weekly maid. Or more precisely, she comes twice a week. She said, as a retired gentleman, he would naturally have a revolver, and if a burglar came in, he could grab it on the spur of the moment.\" [2204] \"When did she tell you that?\" [2205] \"A long time ago. I think it was about six months ago.\" [2206] \"Captain Eastbrook?\" murmured Colonel Corcoran. [2207] \"It's like playing a game of chance,\" said Round, with a pin in her mouth. \"Spin and spin, and each time something different comes up.\" [2208] \"That's true,\" groaned Colonel Corcoran. [2209] \"One day Captain Eastbrook came to the lodge to drop off some books. He might have been putting oil on the door then. Even though he was upfront about going there, unlike Miss Chinchcliff.\" [2210] Miss Maple coughed lightly. \"You'll have to forgive our time, Inspector.\" [2211] Colonel Cordeaux looked at her in confusion. [2212] \"After all,\" Miss Maple said, \"you're a policeman, aren't you? People can't possibly tell the police everything, can they?\" [2213] \"I don't see why not,\" Colonel Cordeaux said, \"unless they want to hide a crime.\" [2214] \"She means butter,\" Roundy said, struggling to crawl around a table leg and pin down a floating piece of paper, \"exchanging butter and corn for a hen, sometimes cream \u2013 even sometimes bacon.\" [2215] \"Show him Miss Blacklock's note,\" Miss Maple said, \"It's been a while, but it reads like a first-rate mystery story.\" [2216] \"Where did I put it? You mean this one, Aunt Jane?\" [2217] Miss Marple took the note and looked at it. [2218] \"Yes,\" she said contentedly. \"That's the one.\" [2219] She handed the note to the Inspector. [2220] Blacklock wrote:\n[2221] I made some inquiries - any time after three o'clock on Thursday. [2222] If there's any for me, leave it in the usual place. [2223] Roundy spat out the pin and laughed. Miss Marple watched the inspector's face. [2224] The vicar's wife rushed to explain: \"Thursday is butter-making day at a farm nearby. They let friends have a bit. Usually Miss Chinchcliffe goes to get it. She's on good terms with the farmers, I suppose because she keeps pigs. But it's all done in secret, you know, like a kind of local barter. One gets cream and then sends something else back - cucumbers or something like that - or adds something else when the pig is killed. Occasionally, an animal meets with an accident and has to be destroyed. Oh, you know the sort of thing. People can't tell the police, of course. I suppose many of these barter transactions are illegal - but nobody is quite sure, because the law is so complicated. But I imagine Miss Chinchcliffe took a pound of cream and slipped it into the usual place. By the way, the usual place is the box under the sideboard where the flour is kept. But there was no flour in it.\" [2225] Cora Dedalus sighed. [2226] \"I'm glad I came to you ladies,\" he said. [2227] \"There used to be cloth coupons,\" said Roundabout, \"which couldn't be sold because that would be seen as dishonest. They couldn't be exchanged for money. But people like Mrs. Bartlett, Mrs. Finch, and Mrs. Higgins, if they liked a nice woolen sweater or a winter coat that wasn't too worn out, would pay for it with a cloth coupon instead of money.\" [2228] \"You had better not say any more,\" said Corridor. \"It's all illegal.\" [2229] \"There shouldn't be such silly laws,\" said Roundabout, and popped the pin back in her mouth. \"Of course, I didn't do it, because Julian didn't like me doing that sort of thing, so I didn't. But I knew all about it, of course.\" [2230] A sense of despair overcame the inspector. [2231] \"It all sounds so pleasant and ordinary,\" he said, \"so amusing and simple.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1496] Miss Mulgrew did not attempt to provide an answer. She looked at her commanding friend with curiosity and admiration, waiting for her to impart wisdom. [1497] \"We know he has a revolver because he fired it,\" Miss Inchclyffe said. \"We also know he has a flashlight because we all saw it - that is, unless we were all victims of collective hypnosis, as explained in 'The Indian Rope Trick' - old Eastbrooke's Indian stories are so boring - of course, that's the only explanation.\" [1498] \"But who would do that?\" [1499] \"Right, you could be one, Mulgrew. According to my memory, you were standing behind the door when the lights went out.\" [1500] 4\n[1501] \"It's quite an unusual thing,\" Colonel Eastbrooke muttered. \"Very unusual, Laura.\" [1502] \"What is it, dear?\" [1503] \"Come to my dressing room for a moment.\" [1504] \"What is it, dear?\" [1505] Mrs. Eastbrooke entered the room, stepping over the open door. [1506] \"Do you remember the revolver I showed you?\" [1507] \"Oh, yes, Archy, a dreadful, disgusting, black thing.\" [1508] \"Yes, a German souvenir. It's in this drawer, isn't it?\" [1509] \"Yes, that's right.\" [1510] \"But it's gone now.\" [1511] \"Archy, that's strange!\" [1512] \"Did you touch it?\" [1513] \"Oh, no, I wouldn't dare touch that awful thing.\" [1514] \"Must be that old hag who did it, then?\" [1515] \"Oh, I wouldn't think so. Mrs. Barton wouldn't do such a thing. Should I ask her?\" [1516] \"No\u2014no, best not to ask. I don't want to get into trouble. Do you remember when I showed it to you?\" [1517] \"Oh, about a week ago. You were muttering about your collar and the laundry room, and then you opened that drawer wide, and there it was, right inside. I even asked you what it was.\" [1518] \"Yes, that's right, about a week ago. You don't remember the exact date, do you?\" [1519] Mrs. Eastbrook paused, her eyelids drooping over her eyes, her sharp mind working. [1520] \"Of course,\" she said, \"it was Saturday. We were supposed to go to the movies, but we didn't.\" [1521] \"Hmm - not before that, right? Tuesday? Wednesday or the week before last?\" [1522] \"No, dear,\" Mrs. Eastbrooke said, \"I remember quite well. It was Saturday, the 30th. Because it seemed like a long time because of that trouble. I remember because it was the day after the robbery at Miss Blacklock's house. Because when I saw your revolver, I remembered the gunshot the night before.\" [1523] \"Ah,\" said Colonel Eastbrooke, \"then I'm relieved.\" [1524] \"Oh, Archy, why?\" [1525] \"Because if my revolver was lost before the shooting incident - then my gun was probably stolen by that Swiss guy.\" [1526] \"But how could he know you had a gun?\" [1527] \"These mob connections are incredibly well-informed. They know things like locations and who lives where.\" [1528] \"You know so much, Archibald.\" [1529] \"Ha, yes, I've seen a few of them. Since you clearly remember seeing my revolver after the burglary, that's fine. The Swiss guy's gun couldn't have been mine, right?\" [1530] \"Of course not.\" [1531] \"It's a relief. I should have gone to the police station, but they would have asked me a lot of embarrassing questions. That's for sure. I don't even have a gun license. Somehow, after the war, people forgot about the rules during peacetime. I kept it as a war memento, not as a weapon.\" [1532] \"Yes, I understand. Of course.\" [1533] \"But the problem is, where the hell did that thing go?\" [1534] \"Maybe Mrs. Bart took it. She seems honest, but after the robbery, she was on edge and might have wanted a gun in her own home. Of course, she wouldn't admit it. I wouldn't even ask, or she'd get angry. So what do we do? This is a big house \u2013 I can't \u2013\"\n[1535] \"Indeed,\" said Colonel Eastbrooke, \"it's best not to mention it.\" [1536] * * *\n[1537] In English, the words \"prinking\" and \"pinking\" sound similar. [1538] Burne Jones (1833-1898) was a Pre-Raphaelite painter. [1539] Chapter 13: Morning in the Village (Continued)\n[1540] Miss Marple walked out of the vicarage and along the alleyway leading to the main street. [1541]She walked briskly with the sturdy walking stick of Mr. Julian Harmon, the vicar. [1542]She passed by the Red Cow store and the butcher's shop, pausing for a moment in front of Mr. Elliott's antique shop. The shop was cleverly situated next to the \"Bluebird\" tea and coffee house, so that when the wealthy patrons stopped by for a cup of good tea and a slice of the so-called \"homemade cake,\" they might be tempted by Mr. Elliott's tastefully decorated window display. [1543]In the circular protruding antique window display, Mr. Elliott showcased items to suit all tastes. Two Watford-made glass wine glasses sat perfectly on a cold wine holder. A desk made of various shapes of walnut wood was unmistakably genuine. A table in the window displayed a variety of cheap door handles and oddities, including a few Dresden-patterned ceramic pieces, two strands of ugly-looking beaded necklaces, a mug with the inscription \"Tribute to Trumbull,\" and a few pieces of Victorian-style silverware. [1544]Miss Marple was intently gazing at the items in the window display. Mr. Elliott, like an old, fat spider, peered out from his web, calculating whether he could possibly catch this newly arrived \"fly.\" [1545]But as he decided that the \"Tribute to Trumbull\" gift was too expensive for the lady living in the vicarage (Mr. Elliott, of course, knew exactly who she was), Miss Marple, through the corner of her eye, saw Miss Dora Bunner enter the \"Bluebird\" coffee house. She decided then and there that she must have a cup of morning coffee to fend off the chill. [1546] Several ladies were already inside the coffee shop, taking a break from their morning shopping. Miss Marple looked around the dimly lit interior of the \"Bluebird\" and pretended to be browsing casually. Suddenly, Miss Bunner's voice called out to her:\n[1547] \"Ah, good morning, Miss Marple. Come and sit here with me. I'm all alone.\" [1548] \"Thank you.\" [1549] Miss Marple gratefully sat down in the hard, blue-painted wooden armchair provided by the \"Bluebird.\" [1550] \"This wind is bitterly cold,\" she complained. \"My legs are rheumatic, so I can't walk fast.\" [1551] \"Ah, I understand. I had sciatica for a year \u2013 it was quite painful most of the time.\" [1552] The two ladies chatted for a while about rheumatism, sciatica, and neuritis. A girl with a severe expression wore a pink apron with a flying bluebird print. She scribbled down their ordered coffee and cake on the menu with a bored look and a yawn. [1553] \"The cakes here are quite good,\" Miss Bunner whispered conspiratorially. [1554] \"I'm interested in the quite pretty girl I met when I left Miss Blacklock's house,\" Miss Marple began. \"I think she said she was a gardener. Is she a local? Hems \u2013 is that her name?\" [1555] \"Ah, yes, Philly Hems. Our 'tenant.' \" Miss Bunner chuckled at her own joke. \"A quiet, well-bred girl, a lady, if you catch my meaning.\" [1556] \"I wonder. I know a Colonel Hayes \u2013 in the Indian cavalry. Perhaps it's her father?\" [1557] \"She's Mrs. Hayes, a widow. Her husband was killed in Sicily or Italy. Of course, it could have been her father.\" [1558] \"I wonder if she has a little romance in her life,\" Miss Marple hinted playfully. \"With the tall young man?\" [1559] \"You mean Patrick? I don't know \u2013\"\n[1560] \"No, I mean the one with glasses. I saw them together.\" [1561] \"Ah, yes, Edmund Swithinham. Shh! There's his mother, Mrs. Swithinham. Honestly, I don't know. Do you think he fancies her? He's such an odd young man\u2014always saying the most off-putting things. He should be quite clever, you know.\u201d Miss Bonner spoke with evident disapproval. [1562] \"Being clever doesn't mean everything,\" Miss Marple shook her head. \"Ah, our coffee has arrived.\" [1563] The girl with the furrowed brow slammed down the coffee cup. Miss Marple and Miss Bonner passed the cake back and forth. \"I'm interested to hear you and Miss Blacklock were at school together. Your friendship must be quite deep.\" [1564] \"Yes, it is,\" Miss Bonner sighed. \"Few people can match Miss Blacklock's loyalty to old friends. Oh, it seems like so long ago. Such a pretty girl, living so carefree. It all seems so sad.\" [1565] Miss Marple, not knowing what \"so sad\" meant, sighed and shook her head. \"Life can be so difficult,\" she murmured softly. [1566] \"To bear the pain bravely,\" Miss Bonner whispered, tears welling up in her eyes, \"I always think of this verse. True endurance, sincere submission. Such courage and endurance should be rewarded, and I always say so. My feelings for Miss Blacklock are not excessive, and she deserves any good reward she receives.\" [1567] \"Money,\" Miss Maple said, \"can make life's path very smooth.\" [1568] She felt safe saying this because she assumed that Miss Bonner was referring to the wealthy life that Miss Blacklock longed for. [1569] However, this statement sparked a different perspective in Miss Bonner. [1570] \"Money!\" she retorted sharply, \"Unless one has had personal experiences, you know, I don't believe anyone can truly understand the meaning of having or not having money.\" [1571] Miss Maple nodded sympathetically, her silver hair bobbing. [1572] Miss Bonner quickly continued, her voice growing more and more animated, her face flushing. \"I often hear people say, 'I'd rather have flowers on the table than no flowers at dinner.' But have they ever gone hungry? They don't know what it's like to truly starve\u2014without nourishment, you see. Bread, a can of soup, a little bit of margarine. The same thing day after day, longing for a plate full of meat and vegetables. Then there's clothing\u2014torn and patched, fearful of showing skin. And job applications\u2014they always say you're too old. Even if you manage to find a job, you're not as well-nourished, so you faint. And then you're back to square one. But rent\u2014always rent\u2014you have to pay it, or you're out on the street. Those days, there wasn't much left over. Pension funds don't last long\u2014really, they don't.\" [1573] \"I understand,\" said Miss Maple gently, her eyes filled with sympathy as she looked at Miss Bonner's trembling face. [1574] \"So I wrote a letter to Letty. I saw her name in the paper. It was for a luncheon to support Milchester Hospital. Miss Letitia Blacklock. It brought back memories of the past, and I hadn't heard from her in years. You know, she was a secretary to a very wealthy man\u2014Mr. Goddard\u2014and she was always a clever girl\u2014the kind who would go far in life. She was one of those people who couldn't be judged by her appearance, but she had that kind of character. I thought\u2014yes, that's what I thought\u2014perhaps she would remember me\u2014the one person I could turn to. You see, we were all girls back then\u2014together at school\u2014they knew me well\u2014they knew I wouldn't ask for help in writing a letter\u2014\"\n[1575] Tears welled up in Miss Bonner's eyes. [1576] \"Then Lotte came and took me away \u2013 said she needed someone to help her. Of course, I was very surprised \u2013 very surprised \u2013 but newspapers do make mistakes. She was so kind \u2013 so full of sympathy \u2013 and remembered everything so well\u2026 I'd do anything for her \u2013 I would. I try hard, but I suppose I sometimes make a mess of things \u2013 my mind isn't what it used to be. I forget things, say silly things. But she's so patient. The best thing about her is that she always pretends I'm useful to her. It's genuine kindness, isn't it?\" [1577] \"Yes, it's genuine kindness,\" Miss Marple said gently. [1578] \"Even after coming to Little Paddocks, you know, I was always worried \u2013 what would happen if \u2013 if Miss Blacklock had an accident, and I was left with nothing? There are so many chances \u2013 cars speeding by \u2013 you can't predict it, can you? But I didn't say anything, but I think she must have guessed something. One day, she suddenly told me she'd leave me a small pension in her will \u2013 and all her lovely furniture. I was over the moon\u2026 and she said, no one takes better care of furniture than I do \u2013 that's true \u2013 I can't bear to see nice china broken, or wet cups left on the table, with marks on them. I do look after her things. Some people \u2013 especially some people \u2013 are so careless \u2013 sometimes worse than careless \u2013\"\n[1579] \"I'm not as stupid as I look,\" Miss Bonner continued, \"I can see, you know, if Miss Blacklock met with an accident, someone \u2013 I won't name names \u2013 but they would benefit. Miss Blacklock is too trusting, maybe. Miss Marple shook her head. [1580] \"I'm not as stupid as I look,\" Miss Bonner continued, \"I can see, you know, if Miss Blacklock met with an accident, someone \u2013 I won't name names \u2013 but they would benefit. Miss Blacklock is too trusting, maybe.\" Miss Marple shook her head. [1581] \"That was a mistake.\" [1582] \"Yes. You and I, Miss Marple, know the world. But dear Miss Blacklock \u2013\" She shook her head. [1583] Miss Marple thought that as a secretary to a big financier, Miss Blacklock should have known better. But perhaps Miss Bunce meant that Miss Letty Blacklock had always been spoiled and therefore didn't understand the depths of human nature. [1584] \"That Patrick!\" Miss Bunce said, with such suddenness and severity that Miss Marple jumped. \"I know he asked her for money at least twice. He pretended to be all broken up about debts and so on. She was too generous. I advised her, and she only said to me, 'He's young, Miss Bunce. Young people ought to have fun.'\" [1585] \"Well, that's true,\" Miss Marple said. \"And he is such a good-looking young man.\" [1586] \"Handsome he may be, but he must have the bearing of a gentleman,\" said Miss Bunner. \"But he is too fond of making fun of people. I think he has had dealings with many girls. I was just one of his playthings - that's all there is to it. He seems not to realize that people have feelings.\" [1587] \"Young people are like that,\" said Miss Marple. [1588] Miss Bunner suddenly leaned in close. [1589] \"You won't say a word, dear?\" she asked, pleading. \"But I can't help feeling that he must have had something to do with this terrible business. I think he knew the young man - and so did Julia. I dared not hint at this to dear Miss Blacklock - but I did, and she scolded me. Of course, it's awkward, because he's her nephew - or at least her cousin. If the Swiss young man committed suicide, Patrick may have been morally responsible, don't you think? I mean, if he made him do it. I'm all at sea over the whole thing. Several people made a fuss about entering the room through another door. That's another thing that bothers me - the inspector said the door had been oiled. Because you see, I saw -\" she suddenly broke off. [1590] Miss Marple weighed her words carefully. [1591] \"It must be so hard for you,\" she said sympathetically. \"You don't want these things to get to the police.\" [1592] \"Exactly,\" said Miss Bunner loudly. \"I couldn't sleep at night, worrying\u2014because you see, one day, I met Patrick in the shrubbery. I was looking for eggs\u2014one that a hen had laid\u2014and there he was, holding a feather and a cup\u2014a greasy cup. When he saw me, he jumped like he'd been caught doing something wrong and said, 'I was wondering what this thing was for.' Of course, he's quick-witted. I'm sure he came up with that excuse on the spot. How else would he have been looking for it in the shrubbery if he hadn't known it was there? Of course, I didn't say anything then.\" [1593] \"No, of course not.\" [1594] \"But I gave him a look, if you understand what I mean.\" [1595] Miss Bunner reached out and took a bite of the salmon-colored cake, her mind elsewhere. \"And another time, I overheard him having a strange conversation with Julia. They seemed to be arguing. He said, 'If I knew you were involved in this kind of thing!' Julia\u2014who's always so calm, you know\u2014said, 'Oh, big brother, what then?' Unfortunately, at that moment, I stepped on that squeaky board, and they saw me. So I asked, 'Are you arguing?' Patrick said, 'I was warning Julia not to continue with this black market deal.' Oh, what a smooth talker, but I'm sure they weren't talking about that at all! If you ask me, I believe Patrick tampered with the table lamp in the drawing room to put out the other lamps, because I distinctly remember it was the shepherdess\u2014not the shepherd boy's lamp. However, the next day\u2014\"\n[1596] She broke off abruptly, her face flushing pink.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1156] \"Unless you give us a chance. Do you think Mrs. Hyde didn't lock the door?\" [1157] \"I think she deliberately didn't lock it.\" [1158] \"What do you mean by that?\" asked Corridock. [1159] \"That young man, he wasn't working alone. No, he knew where to come in and knew the door would be left open for him \u2013 ah, for his convenience!\" [1160] \"What exactly do you mean?\" [1161] \"What good would it do for me to say anything? You won't listen. You'll say I'm a lying poor refugee. You'll say a beautiful British lady, oh, no, she wouldn't lie\u2014she's such a proper Englishwoman\u2014so honest. So you believe her, not me. But I'm telling the truth. Yes, I'll put it this way!\" [1162] She slammed the frying pan onto the stove. [1163] Coraldrake wavered as to whether to take her seriously, for it might just be more of her endless stream of vicious words. [1164] \"We take everything that is told to us seriously.\" [1165] \"I won't tell you anything. Why should I? You're all the same. You persecute poor refugees and look down on us. If I tell you that the young man came to Miss Blacklock a week ago to ask for money, and she sent him away, and, according to what you say, in a huff\u2014if I tell you I heard him talking to Mrs. Hyde\u2014yes, right outside in the pavilion\u2014you'll say I'm making up stories!\" [1166] You might be making it up, Coleridge thought. [1167] But he said aloud, \"You couldn't have heard them talking in the summerhouse.\" [1168] \"You're wrong,\" Mickey triumphed shrilly, \"I went out to pick nettles\u2014they're a nice vegetable, though they don't think so, but I use them for cooking, and don't tell them. I heard them talking. He said, 'But where can I hide?' She said, 'I'll show you.' Then she said, 'Six past one.' At the time, I thought, 'Ah, that's what you're up to, my lady fair! Off to meet your wild man, and bring him into the house.' Miss Blacklock, I thought, she wouldn't like that, she'd throw you out. I thought I'd watch, and see what happened, and then tell Miss Blacklock. But now I know I was wrong. She wasn't planning love with him, but robbery and murder. But you'll say I'm making it up. You'll say, wicked Mickey, I'm going to put you in jail.\" [1169] Coleridge pondered. She might be making it up, but she might not be. [1170] He asked cautiously, \"Can you swear it was Rudolph Sheringham you heard talking?\" [1171] \"Of course I can swear it. I saw him leave the gazebo and cross the main road. Soon, \"Micky said challengingly, \"I went out to see if there were any nice, young nettles.\" [1172] In October, the inspector thought, will there be nice, young nettles? But he was impressed that Micky could come up with a plausible excuse to cover up the undeniable act of eavesdropping. [1173] \"Is that all you heard?\" [1174] \"Miss Bonner, the one with the long nose, she always bossed me around. Micky! Micky! So I had to go. Oh, she was so annoying, always sticking her nose in. She even said she'd teach me how to cook. Pah! She cooks! Whatever she cooks, it tastes like dishwater, dishwater! Dishwater! Dishwater!\" [1175] \"Why didn't you tell me that?\" Coralreck asked sternly. [1176] \"Because I didn't remember at the time \u2013 I didn't remember... It was only later that I realized it was planned \u2013 it was planned with her.\" [1177] \"Are you sure that the person you saw was Mrs. Hyde?\" [1178] \"Oh, yes, I'm sure. Yes, I'm very sure. She's a thief, that Mrs. Hyde. A thief and an accomplice to bandits. She got a job in the garden, but the pay wasn't enough for such a beautiful lady. Not enough. So she planned to rob the kind-hearted Miss Blacklock.\" [1179] \"If someone were to say they saw you talking to Rudi Scherz,\" the Inspector said, observing her closely,\n[1180] \"If someone were to say they saw me talking to him, it would be a lie, a lie, a big lie.\" She said dismissively, \"It's easy to lie behind someone's back, but in Britain, you have to prove it. That's what Miss Blacklock told me, and she's right, isn't she? I didn't talk to the murderer and the thief, and no British police can say I did. You keep going on and on and on, and you want me to make lunch? Go out of my kitchen, please. I have to make a delicate sauce.\" [1181] Kolarik obediently left. His suspicions about Miss Micky had wavered a bit. The story of Mrs. Philips Hedmes was very convincing. Miss Micky might have lied (he thought she did), but he felt there might be a grain of truth in the story. He decided to talk to Mrs. Philips about it. The last time he asked her, he thought she was a quiet, well-bred young woman and had not suspected her. [1182] As he passed through the hall, he opened the wrong door by mistake. Miss Bonner was coming downstairs. She corrected him hastily. [1183] \"Not that door,\" she said. \"That door won't open. It should be the one on the left. Quite confusing, isn't it? So many doors.\" [1184] \"Indeed, quite a few,\" said Kolarik, looking around the narrow hall. [1185] Miss Bonner kindly explained to him one by one. [1186] \"This door leads to the cloakroom, then the wardrobe door, then the dining room door - that one over there. And this one, the one you want to go through, is the ornamental door, then the main entrance to the dining room, then the door to the china cabinet and the door to the conservatory, and at the end is the side door. It's all very confusing, isn't it? Especially these two, so close together, I often get them mixed up. In fact, we used to prop the table against the door, but then we moved it to the wall.\" [1187] Krolikovsky mechanically noticed a horizontal line on the wooden board of the door he had tried to open just now. He realized it was the mark of the former location of the table. A slight ripple passed through his mind, and he asked, \"When did you move it?\" [1188] Fortunately, when asking Miss Bonner, he did not need to give a reason. Miss Bonner was always happy to provide an answer, even if her answers were not very valuable. [1189] \"Let me think... just recently - ten days ago, or two weeks ago.\" [1190] \"Why did you move it?\" [1191] \"I really can't remember, probably something to do with the flowers. I think Felicia put a big vase - she arranged a beautiful bouquet - all autumn colors, lush and so big that you could easily get your hair caught when you walked by. So Felicia said, 'Why not move the table away? Flowers look much better against a blank wall than against a doorboard.' We just had to take down 'Wellington at Waterloo'. It wasn't a painting I particularly liked. Later, we hung it in the corner of the room.\" [1192] \"So, it's not a decorative door then?\" Kolarcok asked, looking at the door. [1193] \"Oh, it's a swing door, if that's what you mean. It leads to the small living room, but since the two living rooms were merged, there was no need to have two doors, so this one was locked.\" [1194] \"Locked?\" Kolarcok gently pushed again, \"Do you mean nailed or locked?\" [1195] \"Ah, locked, I think, and probably hinged.\" [1196] He saw the door latch at the top of the door and tried it. The latch slid back easily - too easily...\n[1197] \"When was the last time this door was opened?\" [1198] \"Oh, I think it was a very long time ago. I haven't opened it since I've been here, I remember that.\" [1199] \"You don't know where the key is?\" [1200] \"There are many keys in the hall drawer, probably in there.\" [1201] Kolarcik followed her, peering into the drawer. Inside were a variety of rusty old-fashioned keys. He scanned them all, picked out one that looked different, and went back to the door. The key fit the lock and turned smoothly. He pushed, and the door slid open silently. [1202] \"Oh, be careful,\" Miss Bonner called. \"There might be something propping the door open, we never open it.\" [1203] \"Is that so?\" the Inspector said. [1204] His face fell, and he spoke in a stern tone: \"This door was opened recently, Miss Bonner, and the hinges have been oiled.\" [1205] She stared at him in disbelief. [1206] \"But who would do that?\" she asked. [1207] \"That's exactly what I intend to find out,\" Colardo said coldly. He pondered: \"An intruder from outside? No\u2014the intruder is here\u2014in this house\u2014that night, the intruder was in the living room\u2026\"\n[1208] Chapter 10: Siblings\n[1209] 1\n[1210] This time, Miss Blacklock paid more attention to what Colardo was saying. He knew her to be a clever woman, and she quickly picked up on the underlying meaning of his words. [1211] \"Indeed,\" she said calmly, \"that does change the situation\u2026 no one has the right to tamper with that door. As far as I know, no one has touched that door.\" [1212] \"You know what that means,\" urged the inspector. \"Anyone in this room that night could have slipped out through that door and sneaked up behind Rudie Schlesinger to shoot you.\" [1213] \"Without anyone seeing or hearing?\" [1214] \"Invisible, like a ghost. Remember, when the lights went out, people were shouting, bumping into each other. The only thing visible after that was the blinding light from the flashlight.\" [1215] Miss Blacklock asked slowly, \"You believe one of these people\u2014one of my ordinary, decent neighbors\u2014slipped out and tried to kill me? Me? But why? For heaven's sake, why?\" [1216] \"I have a feeling, Miss Blacklock, that you know the answer to that question.\" [1217] \"But I don't, Inspector. I can assure you, I don't.\" [1218] \"Well, let's talk about it then. Who gets your money when you're gone?\" [1219] Miss Blacklock answered reluctantly. [1220] \"Patrick and Julia. I left the furniture in this house and a small annuity to Bonnie. In fact, I don't have much to leave. I used to have some German and Italian securities, but they're worthless now. I live on taxes and low-interest investments. I can assure you, I'm not worth being murdered \u2013 I converted most of my money into an annuity a year ago.\" [1221] \"You still have some income, Miss Blacklock, and that money would be inherited by your nephew and niece.\" [1222] \"So Patrick and Julia plotted to kill me? I don't believe it. They're not that desperate.\" [1223] \"Are you sure about that?\" [1224] \"No. I just got the impression from what they told me... but I refuse to suspect them. Some day, maybe, I'll be worth being murdered, but not now.\" [1225] \"What do you mean by saying that someday you'll be worth being murdered, Miss Blacklock?\" Inspector Corridock pressed on. [1226] \"Simply put, someday \u2013 perhaps soon \u2013 I may become a very wealthy woman.\" [1227] \"That sounds interesting. Can you explain?\" [1228] \"Certainly. You may not know, but I've been Randall Goodale's secretary for over twenty years, and we were quite close.\" [1229] Inspector Corrado's interest was piqued. Randall Goodale was a prominent figure in the financial world. He gained fame through his daring speculations and flamboyant publicity stunts. If Corrado wasn't mistaken, he died in 1937 or 1938. [1230] \"I think he lived a long time ago,\" Miss Blacklock said, \"but you've probably heard of him.\" [1231] \"Ah, yes. He was a millionaire, right?\" [1232] \"Oh, much more than that - although his net worth fluctuated. He never shied away from risk, always putting most of the money he earned into new investments, and thus achieving great success.\" [1233] She spoke animatedly, her eyes sparkling with memories. [1234] \"In the end, he was an extremely wealthy man when he died. He had no children, so he left all his assets to his wife, who then left them to me.\" [1235] The inspector's mind was flooded with a vague recollection. [1236] \"The loyal secretary inherits a fortune\" - that was basically what it meant. [1237] \"For the past twelve years or so,\" Miss Blacklock said, her eyes shining slightly, \"I had a very good motive for murdering Mrs. Goddard - but it doesn't help you, does it?\" [1238] \"Goddard - forgive me for asking - did Mrs. Goddard not feel angry about how her husband handled his property?\" [1239] Miss Blacklock now looked distinctly amused. [1240] \"You needn't be so cautious. What you really want to know is whether I was a mistress of Randall Goddard. No, I wasn't. I don't think Randall ever had any romantic feelings for me, and I certainly didn't have any for him. He loved Bella, his wife, and was devoted to her until the end. I think he made that will out of gratitude. You see, inspector, Randall was on the verge of ruin in the early days of his business. He was short of a few thousand pounds cash. He was involved in a big deal, a very exciting deal; just as bold as his usual plans, but he was just short of the cash to make it work. I saved him. I had some money of my own. I trusted Randall, so I sold all my bonds and gave him the money. And it worked. A week later, he was a millionaire.\" [1241] \"From then on, he treated me like a small partner. Ah! Those were the days. I really enjoyed them. But then my father died, leaving me with my only sister, who was severely disabled. I had to give up everything to look after her. Two years later, Randall died. We made a lot of money together, so I wasn't expecting anything from him. But I was very touched, and very proud, to discover that if Bella died before me - she was the sort of person who looked as if she wouldn't live long - I would inherit all his property. I think the poor man didn't know what to do with it. Bella was a very nice, kind person. She lives in Scotland. I haven't seen her for years, just exchanging Christmas cards. You know, just before the war broke out, I took my sister to a sanatorium in Switzerland. She died of tuberculosis.\" [1242] She paused for a moment, then continued, \"I only came back to England a year ago.\" [1243] \"You say you may soon become rich... how soon?\" [1244] \"I learned from the nurse who is taking care of Mrs. Goddard that Bella is dying. Maybe - only a few weeks.\" [1245] She added sadly, \"Now money means very little to me. My income is enough for my simple needs. At one time, I thought of going back into business, enjoying the excitement of it all, but now... oh, never mind, one gets old. But, Inspector, you still see, don't you, that if Patrick and Julia wanted to kill me for money, they wouldn't be in a hurry. They would have to wait a few weeks.\" [1246] \"Yes, Miss Blacklock. But what if you passed away before Mrs. Goddard?\" [1247] \"You know, I never really thought about it. I think... Pip and Emma, I guess...\"\n[1248] Constable Coleridge hesitated, and Miss Blacklock smiled. [1249] \"It sounds crazy, doesn't it? I believe, if I died first, the money would go to Randall's only legitimate heir - his sister Sonia, or something like that. Randall had an argument with his sister. She married someone, but Randall thought the man was a scoundrel.\" [1250] \"Was he really a scoundrel?\" [1251] \"Oh, I have to say, a complete scoundrel. But I believe he must have been a very attractive man to women. He was Greek or Romanian\u2014or something like that\u2014Stanfordthis, Dimitri Stanfordthis.\" [1252] \"Did Randall Goddard remove his sister's name from his will after she married this man?\" [1253] \"Oh, Sonya was already quite wealthy. Randall had given her a lot of money and had tried to prevent her husband from getting any more. But I believe, when the lawyer urged him to make an heir in case I died first, he reluctantly wrote down Sonya's descendants, only because he couldn't think of anyone else and he wasn't the kind of person who would leave money to charity.\" [1254] \"Does Sonya have any legitimate children?\" [1255] \"Yes, Pip and Emma.\u201d She laughed. \"I know it sounds ridiculous. I only know that Sonya wrote a letter to Bella once, asking her to tell Randall that she was very happy. She said she had a pair of twins named Pip and Emma. As far as I know, she never wrote again. But of course, Bella will tell you more.\" [1256] Miss Blacklock thought her statement was quite interesting, but the inspector showed no signs of happiness. [1257] \"The conclusion is,\" he said, \"that if you had been killed that day, at least two people in this world would have inherited a large sum of money. When you say no one had a motive to kill you, Miss Blacklock, you are mistaken. At least two people had an interest. How old are these siblings?\" [1258] Miss Blacklock furrowed her brow.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1045] \"But, of course,\" said Miss Maple mildly, \"they couldn't possibly\u2014in fact\u2014they couldn't possibly have seen anything\u2026\"\n[1046] \"If I understand correctly,\" Miss Marple's cheeks flushed, her eyes sparkled with childlike delight, \"there was no light in the hallway outside - and none on the stairs?\" [1047] \"Yes,\" Coricardo said. [1048] \"So, if there was a man standing at the door, with a powerful flashlight in his hand, shining it into the room, the people inside would see only the flashlight beam, right?\" [1049] \"Yes, only the flashlight beam. I tried it.\" [1050] \"So, that's it,\" Miss Marple said, her face flushed with pleasure, her eyes sparkling with childlike delight. \"There was no light in the hallway outside - and none on the stairs?\" [1051] \"So, when someone says they saw a masked man, they are actually recalling what they saw later when the lights came on, even though they don't realize it. It all fits together, doesn't it? If we assume that Rudi Scherz is - I think the correct term would be 'a scapegoat'?\" [1052] Ridesdale's gaze upon Miss Marple was so surprised that her face reddened even more. [1053] \"I may have used the wrong word,\" she murmured, \"I'm not very fluent in American English - the terms change so quickly. I learned the word 'scapegoat' from a story by Dashiell Hammett. I heard of him from my nephew Raymond, who is one of the 'hardboiled' literary trio. If I'm not mistaken, a 'scapegoat' refers to someone who takes the blame for others. In my opinion, Rudi Scherz seems to fit the bill. He's actually quite foolish, yet greedy and gullible.\" [1054] Ridesdale suppressed a smile. \"You're suggesting that someone persuaded him to shoot wildly at a room full of people with a gun? That's too far-fetched.\" [1055] \"I believe they told him it was a joke,\" Miss Marple said. \"Of course, he was paid for it. That is, he was hired to place an advertisement in the newspaper, to investigate the mansion, and then to arrive there on the night of the incident, wearing a mask and a cloak, pushing open the door, and waving a flashlight while shouting 'Hands up!'\" [1056] \"And then shoot and kill someone?\" [1057] \"No, no,\" Miss Marple said. \"He didn't have a revolver. \" [1058] \"But everyone says \u2013\" Laidlaw began to speak but stopped. [1059] \"Quite right,\" Miss Marple said. \"Even if he had a gun, no one would have seen it. And I believe he didn't. I think that after he shouted 'Hands up', someone quietly came up behind him, put the gun over his shoulder, and fired those