nd then he said he couldn't come, so I got a bit cold towards him because it was his idea, and I don't like standing next to a foreigner. He said it wasn't his fault, and I said he could make up any excuse he wanted, and then he said he was going to pull a prank that night, and he wouldn't have to pay for it, and asked if I wanted a watch. So I asked him what prank he was talking about, and he said not to tell anyone, and there was going to be a gathering somewhere, and he was going to pull off a robbery. He showed me the ad he had placed, and I couldn't help but laugh. He seemed to think it was childish, but British people are like that \u2013 they never grow up \u2013 of course, I asked him what he meant by us, and we argued, but then made up. Later, I saw the news in the paper, and I realized it wasn't a joke, and that Rudi Scherz had shot someone and then shot himself. At the time, my feelings, sir, only you can understand, can't you? \u2013 Oh, I don't know what to do. I thought if I said I knew about it beforehand, it would make me look like I was involved in the whole thing. But when he talked about it, it really seemed like a joke. I swear he meant it that way. I didn't even know he had a revolver. He never said he was going to bring a gun.\" [1076] Chief Inspector Corduk comforted her and then asked the most important question. [1077] \"Did he say who arranged the gathering?\" [1078] But he got nothing. [1079] \"He didn't say who asked him to do it. I think it was just him. He did it himself.\" [1080] \"Did he mention any names? Did he say it was him or her?\" [1081] \"He didn't say anything, just that someone would scream. 'I'll watch them with a smile on my face.' That's what he said.\" [1082] He didn't smile for long, Colodoc thought. [1083] 4\n[1084] \"It's just conjecture,\" Ridestad said as they drove back to Medenham, \"with no basis in theory. Just an old maid's ramblings, don't take it seriously, huh?\" [1085] \"I don't agree, sir.\" [1086] \"The possibility is virtually nil. A mysterious X gentleman suddenly appearing out of the darkness behind our Swiss friend. Where did he come from? Who is he? Where was he before that?\" [1087] \"He could have come in through the side door,\" Cora said. \"Like Schlesinger, perhaps. He could have come in through the kitchen.\" [1088] \"You mean she could have come in through the kitchen?\" [1089] \"Yes, sir. It's a possibility. I never trusted that foreign girl. She gave me the impression of a low-class woman. All that screaming and hysterics \u2013 they may have been an act. She may have been scheming this whole thing, waiting for the right moment to let him in, manipulating the situation, shooting him, and then locking herself in the dining room, picking up a silverware and a deer skin, and starting the screaming act.\" [1090] \"We have evidence to refute that \u2013 ah \u2013 what was his name? Oh, right, Edmund Sweeney was certain that the lock on the door was keyed, and he turned the key to let her out. Are there any other doors that lead to that part of the mansion?\" [1091] \"Yes, there's a door down the stairs that leads to the back staircase and kitchen, but the handle seems to have fallen off three weeks ago and hasn't been replaced yet. During that time, the door couldn't be opened. I must say that this seems plausible. The lock's hinges and both handles are lying on a shelf in the outer hall, covered with thick rust, but of course an expert could still open the door.\" [1092] \"It would be best to check the girl's file to see if her papers are in order. However, in my opinion, the entire argument is just talk.\" [1093] The director looked at his subordinate with a questioning gaze, and Corrado calmly replied, \"I know, sir, and if you believe it's necessary to close the case, then go ahead. But if you let me try a little more, I would be very grateful.\" [1094] To his surprise, the director quietly said with approval, \"Good fellow.\" [1095] \"We need to check the revolver. If this theory holds up, then the gun isn't Shields's. Of course, no one has said that Shields had a revolver up until now.\" [1096] \"I know, boss, but there are plenty of guns made on the European continent. Americans bring their guns home, and our compatriots do the same. You can't make that inference.\" [1097] \"That's reasonable. Are there any other questioning leads?\" [1098] \"There must be a motive. If this theory has any uniqueness, it means that what happened last Friday was not just a joke, nor was it an ordinary robbery, but a cold-blooded intentional murder. Someone tried to kill Miss Blacklock. But why? In my opinion, if there is anyone who knows the answer, it is Miss Blacklock herself.\" [1099] \"I have learned that she is thoroughly opposed to this idea?\" [1100] \"I understand, boss, but this country has plenty of guns made on the European continent. Americans bring their guns home, and our compatriots do the same. You can't make that inference.\" [1101] \"She's definitely against the idea of Rudi Scherz trying to kill her. That's true. There's one more thing, Chief.\" [1102] \"Hmm?\" [1103] \"Someone else might try to do it again.\" [1104] \"That would certainly prove this inference to be correct,\" the Chief said dryly, \"By the way, keep an eye on Miss Maple, okay?\" [1105] \"Miss Maple? Why?\" [1106] \"I reckon she'd be staying with the vicar at Chipping Cleghorn and going to Middlingham twice a week for treatment. There's a Mrs. What's-her-name who's the daughter of an old friend of Miss Maple's. That old girl's got a good intuition. Oh, and I reckon she doesn't have much excitement in her life, so she's always sniffing around, looking for a suspect, to give her some excitement.\" [1107] \"I hope she doesn't come,\" Cora Crook said seriously. [1108] \"Won't come and bother you?\" [1109] \"No, sir, she's a nice old lady. I wouldn't want anything to happen to her... I'm always wondering, I mean, wondering if there's anything else going on in this theory.\" [1110] \"* * *\"\n[1111] [1] Edmund Blair Leighton (1852-1922), British painter, specializing in medieval and Regency-era subjects. [1112] [2] Alma Tadema (1836-1912), British Royal Academy painter, representative of the Pre-Raphaelite movement. [1113] [3] Seven: seven; seventeen: seventeen. [1114] Chapter 9: The Mystery of the Door\n[1115] 1\n[1116] \"I'm sorry to bother you again, Miss Blacklock\u2014\"\n[1117] \"Ah, it's no problem. Considering the investigation was halted for a week, I suppose you want more evidence?\" [1118] Inspector Corridock nodded. [1119] \"Firstly, Miss Blacklock, Rudi Scherz is not the son of the innkeeper of the Alpine Lodge in Monte Largo. His criminal activities began when he was a kitchen helper at a hospital in Bern. Many of the patients there lost small pieces of jewelry. He worked under another name at a winter sports resort, where he was adept at copying two bills in the restaurant, one without the item in question but appearing on the other. The difference, of course, went into his pocket. After that, he worked at a department store in Zurich. During his time there, the store suffered losses due to stolen goods that exceeded the average level. It seems likely that not all of the thefts were the fault of the customers.\" [1120] \"So, he's been known to take small things without permission, then?\" Miss Blacklock said dryly. \"I suppose I was right to say I hadn't seen him before?\" [1121] \"You're quite right \u2014 undoubtedly, you were pointed out to him at the Royal Spa Hotel, so he pretended to recognize you. The Swiss police forced him to leave the country, so he came here with a beautifully forged set of documents and found work at the Royal Spa Hotel.\" [1122] \"Quite a lucrative hunting ground,\" Miss Blacklock commented dryly. \"The expenses are extremely high, and only the very wealthy stay there. I imagine some of them don't care about the bills.\" [1123] \"Yes,\" Inspector Corrado said. \"There's a lot of potential for a big haul there.\" [1124] Miss Blacklock frowned. [1125] \"I see,\" she said. \"But why did he come to Chipping Cleghorn? What makes him think our things are better than those at the Royal Spa Hotel?\" [1126] \"You still maintain that there's nothing particularly valuable in your home, do you?\" [1127] \"Of course not. If there was, I would know. I can assure you, Inspector, we don't have any undiscovered Rembrandt paintings or the like.\" [1128] \"So it seems that Miss Bonner was right, doesn't it? He came to attack you.\" [1129] \"Well, what did I tell you, Letty?\" [1130] \"Oh, nonsense, Bonnie.\" [1131] \"But isn't it true nonsense?\" asked Kroll. \"I think you know that it's not.\" [1132] Blacklock glared at him. [1133] \"We must make this clear. Do you really believe that the young man came here to reveal himself \u2013 and even advertised in the paper so that half the village would be present at a specific time \u2013\"\n[1134] \"Perhaps he didn't mean it that way,\" Miss Bonner interjected impatiently. \"Maybe it was a terrible warning to you, Letty \u2013 that's what I've always thought \u2013 'Wanted for Murder' \u2013 my bones feel cold just thinking about it \u2013 if everything went according to plan, he would have shot you and escaped. And who knows who did it?\" [1135] \"There's some truth in that,\" said Blacklock. \"But \u2013\"\n[1136] \"I knew that ad wasn't a joke, Letty. I said so at the time. Look at Midge\u2014she was scared to death!\" [1137] \"Ah,\" said Corrado, \"Speaking of Midge, I would like to know more about this young woman.\" [1138] \"Her work permit and other documents are all in order.\" [1139] \"I don't doubt that,\" said Corrado stiffly, \"Shelz's papers seem to be in order.\" [1140] \"But why would Rudie Shelz want to kill me?\" asked Inspector Corrado. \"You don't seem to be planning to explain that, do you, Inspector Corrado?\" [1141] \"There may be someone behind Shelze,\" said Corradino slowly, \"have you thought of that?\" [1142] He used a metaphorical expression, although the thought had occurred to him that if Miss Maple's reasoning was correct, the literal meaning of his words would also hold true. In any case, this did not impress Miss Blacklock, who still looked suspicious. [1143] \"The same question,\" she said, \"why should anyone want to kill me?\" [1144] \"I would like you to answer that, Miss Blacklock.\" [1145] \"But I can't! It's obvious. I have no enemies. I've always got on well with my neighbors. I don't know anyone's secrets. The whole idea is ridiculous! If you're suggesting that Micky is involved, that's equally absurd. Miss Bonner told you that Micky was terrified when she saw the notice in the paper. In fact, she wanted to pack her bags and leave right away.\" [1146] \"That could be a clever move on her part. She might know you'd insist she stay.\" [1147] \"Well, if that's what you think, then you can find the answer to any question. But I can assure you that if Midge hated me without reason, she could have easily poisoned my food. But I'm sure she wouldn't go to such lengths. The whole idea is ridiculous. I believe you have a phobia against foreigners. Midge may be a liar, but she's not a cold-blooded killer. If you want to interrogate her, go ahead. But if she storms off in anger or locks herself in her room crying, I'll have to ask you to cook dinner. Mrs. Harmon will be bringing an elderly lady over for tea this afternoon, and I want Midge to make some small cakes \u2013 but I suppose you'll make her angry.\" [1148] \"Perhaps it's a clever ploy on her part. She might know you'd insist she stay.\" [1149] 2\n[1150] Corrado went to the kitchen. He asked the same questions again, and the answers remained the same. [1151] Yes, she locked the front door soon after 4 o'clock. No, she didn't usually do this, but that afternoon she was very tense due to \"the terrible advertisement.\" [1152] \"Mrs. Hymer said she locked the door when she came in at 5:30.\" [1153] \"Ah, you believe her \u2013 oh, yes, you believe her\u2026\"\n[1154] \"Do you think we shouldn't believe her?\" [1155] \"What does it matter what I think? You won't believe me.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[792] \"No need to be formal,\" he said with a smile. \"I'll try not to take up too much of your time.\" [793] \"Thank you.\" [794] \"Just a statement. At what time did you go inside after finishing your work last night?\" [795] \"Around 5:30 PM. I stayed a bit longer to water the greenhouse, about twenty minutes more than usual.\" [796] \"Which door did you enter through?\" [797] \"The side door, with the chicken coop and duck pen in between it and the driveway. It saves me from going around in a circle and not getting dirt on the front porch. Sometimes I get pretty dirty.\" [798] \"Is that the way you always come in?\" [799] \"Yes.\" [800] \"The door was open?\" [801] \"Yes. It's always open in the summer, and now it's closed, but not locked. We come and go that way, and I lock it when I come in.\" [802] \"You've always done that?\" [803] \"I have for the whole week. You know, it gets dark at six. Miss Blacklock sometimes goes out to put the chickens and ducks to bed, but she often uses the kitchen door.\" [804] \"Are you sure you locked the side door last night?\" [805] \"I'm very sure.\" [806] \"Good, Mrs. Haymes. So, what did you do after you went inside?\" [807] \"I took off my muddy shoes, went upstairs to take a bath and change. Then I went downstairs and found that there seemed to be a party. It was then that I found the strange notice.\" [808] \"Now, please describe the robbery incident in detail.\" [809] \"Well, all the lights suddenly went out.\" [810] \"Where were you at that time?\" [811] \"I was by the fireplace. I was looking for my lighter, which I thought I had placed there. After the lights went out, everyone started laughing. Then the door was pushed open, and a man shone a flashlight at us and waved a revolver, telling us to raise our hands.\" [812] \"And you just did that?\" [813] \"Well, no. I thought it was a joke. I was tired and didn't feel like raising my hands.\" [814] \"So, you found the whole thing boring?\" [815] \"Yes, I did. But then the gun went off. The sound was deafening, and I was terrified. The flashlight was waving around, and then it fell to the ground and went out. Then, Micky started screaming, like a pig being slaughtered.\" [816] \"Did you find the flashlight light too bright?\" [817] \"No, not really. It was pretty strong, but it just shone on Miss Bonner for a while. You know, she looked like a turnip lamp [2]. She was really pale, with her mouth wide open and her eyes almost popping out of her head.\" [818] \"Did the man move the flashlight?\" [819] \"Yes, he was shining it all around the room.\" [820] \"As if he was looking for someone?\" [821] \"I don't think so.\" [822] \"What happened next, Mrs. Hyde?\" [823] Philippa Hyde frowned. [824] \"Oh, it was a complete mess. Edmund Swetnam and Patrick Simmons lit their lighters, and the two of them went out to the hallway, with us following behind. Someone opened the door to the dining room \u2013 the fuse hadn't blown, and the lights were still on \u2013 and Edmund Swetnam slapped Midge hard, silencing her scream. After that, things weren't so bad.\" [825] \"Did you see the body?\" [826] \"Yes.\" [827] \"Do you know him? Have you ever met him before?\" [828] \"Never.\" [829] \"Do you think his death was accidental or a suicide?\" [830] \"I have no idea.\" [831] \"Have you ever seen him come to the manor before?\" [832] \"No. I believe he must have come in the morning, when I wasn't here. I'm not home during the day.\" [833] \"Thank you, Mrs. Hammers. Do you have any valuable jewelry? Rings, bracelets, things like that?\" [834] Phyllida shakes her head. [835] \"My engagement ring \u2013 a couple of brooches.\" [836] \"Also, do you know of anything particularly valuable in the manor?\" [837] \"No. I mean, there are some quite decent silverware, but nothing particularly unusual.\" [838] \"Thank you, Mrs. Hedmes.\" [839] 2\n[840] Korduk returns along the same path, and in the garden, he collides with a tall, rosy-cheeked lady with a tightly laced corset. [841] \"Good morning,\" she snapped, \"What are you doing here?\" [842] \"Mrs. Lucas? I'm Inspector Calderon.\" [843] \"Oh, I see. My apologies. I don't like strangers wandering around the garden and wasting the gardener's time. But I understand you're on official business.\" [844] \"Indeed.\" [845] \"Can I ask, will the violence that occurred at Miss Blacklock's last night happen again? Was it a gang-related incident?\" [846] \"We are relieved to inform you, Mrs. Lucas, that it was not the work of a gang.\" [847] \"There are so many robberies these days, the police have become lax.\" [848] \"I need her statement as a witness.\" [849] \"I don't suppose you can wait until 1 pm to question her? It would be fairer to ask her directly rather than using my time...\u201d\n[850] \"I need to return to headquarters urgently.\" [851] \"These days, no one can expect sympathy or to work a full day without any interruptions. Arriving late, taking a half-hour break at ten o'clock, having tea at eleven, and leaving as soon as it starts to rain. When it's time to mow the lawn, the lawnmower breaks down. With five to ten minutes left before the end of the day, she's nowhere to be found.\" [852] \"Mrs. Hedman told me that she left at the usual time yesterday, but worked until five twenty.\" [853] \"Oh, she would say that. Mrs. Hedman is quite dedicated to her work, despite a few days when I couldn't find her anywhere. She was born a lady, of course, and everyone feels responsible for helping these poor widows. Because of the war, they were left alone so young. It's not easy, but there are long breaks at school and arrangements made for that, which means she gets extra time off. I told her about the summer camps, which are really great, and she can send her children there to play and have fun. They don't need to come home during the summer holidays.\" [854] \"But Mrs. Hedman didn't like the suggestion?\" [855] \"That girl is as stubborn as a mule. Just last year, I had the grass cut on the tennis court and the lines painted every day. But Old Ash painted the lines crooked. No one thought about whether it was convenient for me!\" [856] \"I guess Mrs. Hemmings's wages are lower than most people's?\" [857] \"Of course. What else would she expect?\" [858] \"I'm sure she doesn't ask for anything else,\" said Colquhoun. \"Have a nice morning, Mrs. Lucas.\" [859] 3\n[860] \"It's terrible,\" said Miss Sweetnam with a smile. [861] \"Quite\u2014quite\u2014horrible. I mean, the News office should have been more careful when accepting advertisements. I found the notice very suspicious at the time, didn't I, Edmund?\" [862] \"Do you remember what you were doing when the lights went out, Mrs. Swetnam?\" [863] \"It brought back memories of my old nurse. Where was Moses when the light went out? The answer, of course, is 'in the dark.' We were all there last night, wondering what would happen. Then, as you know, there was that terrifying feeling when everything went black. The door opened\u2014there was just a vague figure standing there, a revolver, a blinding light, and a threatening voice saying, 'Money or your life!'\" [864] \"Ah, I've never been so happy. Then, about a minute later, it was absolutely dreadful, a real bullet whizzing past our ears! It was like a charge of a storming party in wartime.\" [865] \"Where were you standing or sitting, Mrs. Swetnam?\" [866] \"Let me think, I was\u2014I was talking to who, Edmund?\" [867] \"I have no idea, Mom.\" [868] \"I was asking Miss Chinchcliff about giving the chickens fish liver oil on cold days, or was it Mrs. Harmon\u2014no, she hadn't arrived yet. I think I was talking to Colonel Eastbrook, and I thought it was very dangerous to build an atomic bomb research station in England. It should be built on a deserted island to avoid radiation leaks.\" [869] \"Do you remember if you were standing or sitting?\" [870] \"Is that important, Inspector? I was by the window, or near the fireplace, because I was near the clock when it struck.\" [871] \"You said the flashlight blinded you so you couldn't see. Was the flashlight aimed directly at you?\" [872] \"It was shining right in my eyes. I couldn't see anything.\" [873] \"Was the man holding the flashlight still or was he shining it on people one by one?\" [874] \"Oh, I'm not too sure. How did he do it, Edmund?\" [875] \"The flashlight shone on us one by one, as if to see what we were doing, I guess, to make sure we weren't going to rush him.\" [876] \"Where exactly were you at the time, Mr. Swetnam?\" [877] \"I was talking to Julia Simmons. We were both standing in the middle of the room \u2013 the long one.\" [878] \"Was everyone in that room? Was there anyone in the room at the end of the hallway?\" [879] \"Philipa Hemmings came in from there. She was by the fireplace on the far side. I think she was looking for something.\" [880] \"Do you think the third shot was a suicide or an accident?\" [881] \"I have no idea. The man seemed to turn around suddenly and then stumbled and fell to the ground\u2014but everything was in a mess. You have to understand that nothing could really be seen clearly. Then the refugee girl started calling out from a distance.\" [882] \"I know you unlocked the door to the dining room to let her out, right?\" [883] \"Yes.\" [884] \"Was the door definitely locked from the outside?\" [885] Edmund looked at him curiously. [886] \"Of course. What is it, you don't think \u2013\"\n[887] \"I just want to clarify the facts. Thank you, Mr. Sveta\u0148\u00e1k.\" [888] 4\n[889] Detective Kolar had to spend a long time at Colonel Eastbrook's and his wife's. He had to patiently listen to a lengthy discourse on the psychology of the case. [890] \"Psychological methods \u2013 these are the only important things now,\" the colonel told him. \"You have to understand the criminal. For someone with much more experience than me, the entire trap is obvious. Why did he place the advertisement? Psychological reasons. He wanted to promote himself \u2013 to get everyone's attention. He had been ignored, perhaps the hotel staff looked down on him because he was a foreigner, or maybe a girl had rejected him. Nowadays, what kind of idols are in movies \u2013 gangsters \u2013 tough guys? Great, so he decided to be a tough guy. Violent robbery. A mask? A revolver? But he still needs an audience \u2013 he must have an audience. So he arranges an audience. And then, the moment of climax, his character loses control \u2013 he's not just a thief, but a murderer. He fires \u2013 aimlessly \u2013\"\n[891] Inspector Corrado interrupted at this point. \"You said 'aimlessly,' Colonel Eastbrook. So you think he didn't aim at a specific target \u2013 say, Miss Blacklock?\" [892] \"No, not at all. He just slipped, as I said, aimlessly. That's what got him into trouble. The bullet hit someone \u2013 actually just grazed them, but he didn't know that. He suddenly realized that all this \u2013 these tricks he was playing \u2013 were real. He fired the revolver \u2013 possibly killed someone \u2013 and that pushed him over the edge. So, in a blind panic, he turned the revolver on himself.\" [893] Colonel Eastbrook paused, cleared his throat with satisfaction, and continued triumphantly, \"Clear as day, that's what happened, clear as day.\" [894] \"It's all so interesting,\" said Colonel Eastbrook's wife. \"You know the ins and outs of the whole thing, Archibald.\" [895] Her voice was warm with admiration. [896] Chief Inspector Coradino also found this explanation impressive, but he did not express his approval enthusiastically. [897] \"Where were you in the room, Colonel Eastbrook, when the gun was fired?\" [898] \"My wife and I were standing by the table with the flowers in the middle.\" [899] \"I held your arm, didn't I, Archie? I was absolutely terrified, so I had to hold you.\" [900] \"Poor kitten,\" the colonel jested. [901] 5\n[902] Inspector Corradino had a hard time finding Miss Chickering in the pig pen. [903] \"Pigs are nice animals,\" Miss Chickering said, scratching the pink wrinkled back of the pig, \"they grow up nicely, won't they? By Christmas, they'll be ready to make excellent bacon. Now, what can I do for you? I've told your men that I have no idea who the man was last night. I've never seen him wandering or loitering around here. Our Mrs. Mop says he came from a big hotel in Medenham. Why didn't he just rob him on the road? He could have gotten more that way.\" [904] This was undeniable - Inspector Corradino began the questioning. [905] \"Where were you exactly when the incident occurred?\" [906]\u201cIncident! That reminds me of the days of the air raids. I can tell you I've seen my share of incidents. Where was I when the shooting started? You want to know that, do you?\u201d\n[907]\u201cYes.\u201d\n[908]\u201cRight up against the fireplace, praying to God for a drink.\u201d Miss Chickiff answered without hesitation. [909]\u201cDo you think the bullet was fired randomly or intentionally at someone?\u201d\n[910]\u201cYou mean at Letty Blacklock? Hell, how would I know? It's hard to recall the exact impressions or what really happened after all this. I just know all the lights went out, and a flashlight was shone in our faces, making us dizzy. Then someone fired a shot, and I thought, 'If that damn fool Patrick Simmons had loaded his revolver and was playing around with it, someone would get hurt. '\u201d\n[911] \"You thought it was Patrick Simmons at the time?\" [912] \"Well, it could be. Edmund Swetnam is sensible and writes books, he wouldn't play pranks. Colonel Eastbrooke wouldn't find this amusing either. But Patrick is a wild child. However, I should apologize for this thought.\" [913] \"Did your friend also think it could be Patrick?\" [914] \"Muggleton? You'd better ask her yourself. You won't get much out of her. She's in the orchard. I'll call her over if you like.\" [915] Miss Chickering raised her voice and shouted, \"Hey! Call you, Muggleton...\"\n[916] \"Coming...\" came a faint reply. [917] \"Come here quickly - it's the police.\" [918] Miss Muggleton rushed up, breathless. She had let down her skirts and her hair had slipped out of her too-small net. Her round, pleasant face glowed. [919] \"Is it from Scotland Yard?\" she gasped, \"I wouldn't have left home if I'd known.\" [920] \"We haven't notified Scotland Yard yet, Miss Muggleton. I'm a superintendent from Milchester.\" [921] \"Oh, I'm sure that's all right,\" Miss Mulgrew said vaguely. \"Have you found any clues?\" [922] \"What were you doing at the time of the crime, Miss Mulgrew?\" Miss Inchcliff asked, and winked at Cordeux. [923] \"Oh, my goodness,\" Miss Mulgrew gasped. \"Of course, I should have been prepared. Alibi, of course. Let me think\u2026 I was with everyone.\" [924] \"You weren't with me,\" Miss Inchcliff said. [925] \"Oh, my goodness, was I not? Yes, of course not. I was admiring the chrysanthemums. Such a pitiful species, really. Then everything happened \u2013 I mean, I didn't realize what was happening \u2013 my meaning is, I didn't know such a thing would happen. I didn't even think the revolver was real \u2013 everything was so awkward in the dark, and that dreadful scream. I was completely mistaken, you know. I thought she was being murdered \u2013 I mean, the refugee girl. I thought she was being cut her throat in the hall. I didn't know it was him \u2013 I mean, I didn't even know there was a man. I only heard one voice, you know, saying 'Please raise your hands.'\" [926] \"Hands up!\" Miss Chinchcliff corrected, \"There was no 'please' about it.\" [927] \"I was actually quite enjoying myself until the girl started screaming, and now that I think about it, it's quite horrifying. Being plunged into darkness was bad enough, and I felt hurt, terribly hurt. What else do you want to know, Inspector?\" [928] \"Nothing else,\" Inspector Coradine said thoughtfully, looking at Miss Mulgrew, \"I believe that's all.\" [929] Her friend laughed shortly and sharply. [930] \"He got you good, Miss Mulgrew,\" Coradine said. [931] \"I believe, Miss Quincannon,\" Miss Mulgrew replied, \"that if I knew, I would tell you everything.\" [932] \"That's not what he wants to hear,\" Miss Quincannon interjected. [933] She looked at the inspector. \"If you're going to visit people in their homes, I think you'll want to see the vicar next. You might learn something from her. Mrs. Harmon may seem a bit muddled \u2013 but I occasionally find her quite astute. Anyway, she knows some things.\" [934] As the inspector and Inspector Fletcher strode away, Amy Mulgrew suddenly gasped, \"Oh, Miss Quincannon, have I done terribly? I was so flustered!\" [935] \"Not at all,\" Miss Quincannon smiled, \"Overall, I must say you did quite well.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[265] \"That must have been a wonderful world,\" Julia said with a reverential tone. [266] Miss Blacklock smiled. \"Looking back, I do think so. But, to be fair, I am getting old and it's natural to prefer my own era. But you young people shouldn't think that way.\" [267] \"If I were in the past, I might just stay at home, tending to flowers and writing notes... but why did people in the past write notes? Who did they write to?\" [268] \"They wrote to the people you would call now, Julia,\" Miss Blacklock's eyes sparkled, \"I doubt you even know how to write, do you?\" [269] \"Not like the way I discovered that interesting 'Guide to Correspondence' book that taught you how to politely refuse a widower's proposal.\" [270] \"I doubt you would enjoy staying at home as much as you think,\" Miss Blacklock said, \"Women in the past had family responsibilities, you know.\u201d Her voice became flat, \"But I know little about that. Myself and Bonnie,\u201d she smiled affectionately at Dora Bunner, \"went out into the world very early.\" [271] \"Yes, that's right,\" Miss Bunner agreed. \"I can't forget those mischievous children. Of course, Letty was smart, she was a highly educated woman, a secretary to a prominent financier.\" [272] The door opened, and Felicia Hyms walked in. She was tall and attractive, but her face looked tired. She looked around the room in surprise. [273] \"Hello,\" she said. \"Is there a party? No one told me.\" [274] \"Of course,\" Patrick exclaimed. \"Our Felicia doesn't know. I'll bet she's the only one in Chipping Cleghorn who doesn't know.\" [275] Felicia looked at him quizzically. [276] \"Look, everyone,\" Patrick waved his hand dramatically. \"A crime scene!\" [277] Philippa Hammers looked thoroughly confused. [278] \"Here,\" Patrick pointed to the two large bowls of chrysanthemums. \"These are wreaths, and these trays of cheese straws and olives are for the funeral feast.\" [279] Philippa looked at Miss Blacklock with confusion. [280] \"Is this a joke?\" she asked. \"I'm always slow to understand jokes.\" [281] \"This is a very poor joke,\" Dora Bunner emphasized, \"I don't like it at all.\" [282] \"Show her the notice,\" Miss Blacklock instructed. \"I must go and shut up the ducks. It's getting dark, and people will be arriving soon.\" [283] \"I'll do it,\" Philippa said. [284] \"No, dear, you've been busy all day.\" [285] \"I'll do it, Aunt Letty,\" Patrick volunteered. [286] \"No, you're not going,\" Miss Blacklock said firmly, \"last time you didn't even latch the door properly.\" [287] \"I'll go, Aunt Letty, dear,\" Miss Bonner called, \"really, I'd be glad to. I'll just put on my high boots \u2013 where did I put my woolen vest?\" [288] But at this point, Miss Blacklock had already smiled and left the room. [289] \"Never mind, Bonnie,\" Patrick said. \"Aunt Letty is so efficient, she won't tolerate anyone doing things for her. She really likes to do everything herself.\" [290] \"She does like that,\" Julia said. [291] \"I've never seen you offer to help with anything,\" her brother said. [292] Julia smiled lazily. [293] \"You said yourself, Letty Aunt likes to do things herself,\" she pointed out, \"and besides,\" she extended a slender leg clad in sheer stockings, \"I'm wearing my best socks.\" [294] \"Dying in silk stockings!\" Patrick exclaimed dramatically. [295] \"Not silk\u2014nylon, you idiot.\" [296] \"Nylon doesn't sound classy enough,\" he said. [297] \"No one could do us a favor,\" Philippa complained loudly, \"tell me why everyone is so obsessed with death?\" [298] For a moment, everyone wanted to point out the newspaper to her, but couldn't find it as Mickey had taken it into the kitchen. [299] A few minutes later, Miss Blacklock returned. [300] \"All set,\" she said cheerfully, glancing at the clock, \"6:20. Someone will be here soon - unless I'm completely mistaken about my neighbors.\" [301] \"I can't see why anyone would come,\" Philippa looked perplexed. [302] \"Can't you, dear?... I dare say you can't. But most people are much better than you at it.\" [303] \"Philippa's attitude towards life is indifferent and uncaring,\" Julia commented sharply. [304] Philippa made no response to this. [305] Miss Blacklock looked around the living room. Midge had placed sherry and three plates on the table in the center of the room, with olives, cheese straws, and some odd little treats. [306] \"Patrick, if you don't mind, would you bring the tray - and the table, if you like - from the corner to the bay window in the other room? After all, I'm not having a party! I haven't invited anyone, and I don't want people to think I'm expecting them to turn up.\" [307] \"Are you trying to hide your foresight, Aunt Letty?\" [308] \"You're right, Patrick. Thank you, my dear child.\" [309] \"Now we can all pretend to be having a quiet evening at home,\" said Julia, \"and then be taken by surprise when people arrive.\" [310] Miss Blacklock picked up the bottle of sherry and stood there, hesitatingly holding it. [311] \"There's more than half a bottle, so it should be enough,\" Patrick reassured her. [312] \"Ah, yes, that's right\u2026\" she faltered, her face flushing slightly. [313] \"Patrick, could you please\u2026 there's an unopened bottle in the cabinet in the kitchen. Bring it over with the opener. We\u2014we should drink something new. This one has been open for a while.\" [314] Patrick went off on his mission without a word, returning with the new bottle and opener. As he placed the bottle in the tray, he curiously looked up at Miss Blacklock. [315] \"You seem quite serious about this, dear?\" he whispered. [316] \"Oh, my goodness!\" Miss Blacklock exclaimed in surprise. \"Truly, Letty, you're not thinking of\u2026\"\n[317] \"Shh,\" Miss Blacklock said quickly. \"The doorbell rang. You see, my intuition was right.\" [318] 2\n[319] Midge opened the living room door and let in Colonel Eastbrook and his wife. Her way of announcing the guests was unorthodox. [320] \"Colonel Eastbrook and his wife are here to see you.\" She casually announced. [321] Colonel Eastbrooke was a jovial and easygoing man who brushed off the slight embarrassment with ease. [322] \"We just happened to be passing by and thought we'd drop in,\" he said, and Julia couldn't help but chuckle. \"The night is so lovely, isn't it? I noticed you have the central heating on, ours is still off.\" [323] \"Your flowers are just beautiful,\" Colonel Eastbrooke's wife said, flattering them, \"so charming!\" [324] \"They're just some dried twigs and leaves, really,\" Julia replied. [325] Colonel Eastbrooke's wife greeted Phyllida Hemsley with an overly friendly air, subtly indicating that she knew Phyllida was not a farmhand. [326] \"How's Mrs. Lucas' garden doing?\" she asked, \"Do you think it can be revived? It was completely neglected during the war\u2014and then only one gardener was hired, that awful old man Ash who did nothing but sweep up leaves and plant cabbages.\" [327] \"It's still worth the effort,\" Phyllida answered, \"just needs a bit of time.\" [328] Micky opened the door and announced, \"The ladies from the Gravel Pits are here.\" [329] \"Good evening,\" Miss Inchclyde strode over, grabbed Miss Blacklock's hand and shook it firmly, \"I told Muggleton, 'Let's pay a visit to the Gravel Pits!' I wanted to ask you about your ducks' eggs.\" [330] \"It's getting so dark so early, isn't it?\" Miss Muggleton said nervously to Patrick, \"These chrysanthemums are just beautiful!\" [331] \"All leaves and no flowers,\" Julia said. [332] \"Why can't you just do as you're told?\" Patrick whispered to her. [333] \"You're running the central heating, aren't you?\" Miss Ingham said reproachfully. \"It's too early.\" [334] \"It always gets so damp in this house at this time of year,\" Miss Blacklock replied. [335] Patrick raised his eyebrows, silently indicating with a gesture to offer sherry, but Miss Blacklock replied with a signal: \"Too early.\" [336] She addressed Colonel Eastbrooke:\n[337] \"Did you import bulbs from Holland this year?\" [338] The door opened again, and Mrs. Swetnam came in with Edmund, looking sheepish, followed by a glum-faced Edmund. [339] \"We're here!\" Mrs. Swetnam said cheerfully, and with a blatant curiosity she looked around. Then she suddenly felt awkward and continued, \"I just thought I'd drop in and ask if you happened to want a kitten, Miss Blacklock? Our cat is about to\u2014\"\n[340] \"To be sent to the nest of a vigorous tomcat,\" Edmund said, \"and I think it will be terrible. I've warned you!\" [341] \"It's a wonderful mouser,\" Mrs. Sweetnam hastened to say, adding, \"The chrysanthemums are looking so well!\" [342] \"You're using a central heating system, aren't you?\" Edmund said with a tone of discovery. [343] \"Why do people all sound like gramophones?\" Julia muttered. [344] \"I don't like that news,\" Colonel Eastbrook said to Patrick, \"I don't like it at all. If you ask me, I'd say war is inevitable, absolutely inevitable.\" [345] \"I never pay attention to the news,\" Patrick replied to him. [346] The door opened again, and Mrs. Harmon walked in. [347] She pinned her tattered hat back, as if trying to create some sort of fashion effect, and also changed out of her usual cardigan into a rumpled, frayed-edged jacket. [348] \"Good afternoon, Miss Blacklock,\" she said brightly, \"Am I too late for the murder?\" [349] 3\n[350] Sighs of anticipation could be heard all around. Julia chuckled appreciatively, Patrick scowled, and Miss Blacklock smiled at the last guest. [351] \"Julian was furious that he couldn't come,\" said Mrs. Harmon. \"He loves murder. That's why his sermon last Sunday was so good\u2014though I shouldn't say so, since he's my husband\u2014but it was, wasn't it? Much better than usual. And it was all because of that book, 'The Hat Trick of Death.' Have you read it? The girl at Buzzer's Bookshop held it for me. The plot is so intriguing. You think you know who the murderer is, and then suddenly, the whole thing turns on its head, and there are four or five possible culprits. Well, I left the book in the study one day, and Julian picked it up while he was preparing his sermon, and he got so engrossed in it that he had to rush his sermon and just jot down what he wanted to say, without any of his usual erudite references\u2014and, of course, it was much better that way. Oh, dear, I'm going on and on. But you must tell me when the murder starts?\" [352] Miss Blacklock glanced at the clock on the mantelpiece. [353] \"If it's to start,\" she said cheerfully, \"it should be any moment now. It's only a minute to six thirty. Let's have a sherry while we wait.\" [354] Patrick nipped across the hall. [355] Miss Blacklock went to the table by the hall, where the cigarette box was. [356] \"I'd love a sherry,\" said Mrs. Harmon, \"but what do you mean by 'if'?\" [357] \"Well,\" said Miss Blacklock, \"I'm as much in the dark as you are. I don't know what -\"\n[358] Suddenly, the clock on the mantelpiece struck, and she fell silent. The chimes were sweet and everyone fell quiet, no one moved. [359] Everyone stared at the clock. [360] The chimes began at the quarter past six position and continued until they reached three-quarters past. And just as the last chime faded away, all the lights went out. [361] 4\n[362] In the darkness, there was only the sound of excited breathing and the murmurs of approval from the women. [363] \"It's begun,\" cried Mrs. Harmon, ecstatically. \"Oh, how detestable!\" moaned Dora Bunner. \"Good heavens!\" said others. \"It's awful! It's awful! It gives me gooseflesh. Where are you, Archibald? What am I supposed to do? Oh, dear--I've stepped on your foot. I'm terribly sorry.\" [364] Suddenly, there was a creak, and the door slid open. A beam of intense light flashed around the room. A man's voice, hoarse and nasal, echoed through the room, instantly reminding everyone of those pleasant afternoons spent in the cinema:\n[365] \"Hands up! I said, hands up!\" the man shouted. [366]Hands raised high and willingly over their heads. [367]\"Isn't this thrilling?\" a woman whispered, \"I'm so excited.\" [368]Unexpectedly, a revolver fired twice. The sound of the bullets shattered the joyful atmosphere in the room. Suddenly, it was no longer a game, and someone screamed...\n[369]The shadow at the door turned abruptly, seeming to hesitate for a moment. A third bullet was fired, and the shadow stumbled before collapsing to the ground. The flashlight fell, and the light disappeared. [370]Darkness descended again. Then, with a characteristic creak of Victorian-era craftsmanship, the living room door slid gently back into place, finally clicking shut. [371] 5\n[372] The living room was in chaos, and everyone spoke at once. [373] \"The light.\" [374] \"Can you find the switch?\" [375] \"Who has a lighter?\" [376]\"Oh, I hate this, I hate it!\" [377]\"But those gunshots were real!\" [378]\"He was using a real revolver.\" [379]\"Was it a burglar?\" [380]\"Ah, Arch, I want to leave here.\" [381] \"Who has a lighter, please?\" [382] Then, almost at the same time, two lighters clicked, producing a weak yet steady flame. [383] Everyone blinked and looked around, staring at each other in terror. Miss Blacklock leaned against the arcade wall, her hand covering her cheek. Only a faint outline of something dark could be seen trickling from between her fingers. [384] Colonel Eastbrook cleared his throat and stepped forward spontaneously. [385] \"Try the switch, Swetnam,\" he ordered. [386] Edmund, standing near the door, turned the switch up and down as instructed. [387] \"The main switch is off, or the fuse is blown,\" said the colonel. \"Who's making all the noise?\" [388] A woman's scream echoed from behind the closed door, growing louder and more shrill, accompanied by the sound of fists pounding on the door. [389] Dora Bunner had been quietly crying, and now she blurted out, \"It's Micky. Someone's trying to kill Micky...\"\n[390] \"No such luck,\" Patrick muttered. [391] \"We need to get some candles,\" said Miss Blacklock. \"Patrick, please\u2014\"\n[392] The Colonel was opening the door. He and Edmund, holding a flickering lighter, stepped into the hall and almost tripped over a body lying on the ground. [393] \"Looks like he's been knocked down,\" said the Colonel. \"Where's that screeching woman?\" [394] \"In the dining room,\" said Edmund. [395] The dining room was on the other side of the hall. Someone was pounding on the boards, shrieking. [396] \"She's locked in,\" Edmund said, bending down. He turned the key, and Midge burst out like a tiger out of thin air. [397] The dining room lights were still on. The light faintly illuminated Midge, who looked terrified and kept screaming. The amusing thing was that she had been washing silverware, so she still held a piece of deer skin and a large frying pan. [398] \"Quiet, Midge,\" Miss Blacklock said. [399] \"Stop screaming,\" Edmund said, but Midge didn't stop screaming, so he stepped forward and slapped her sharply. Midge gasped and choked, finally quieting down. [400] \"Get some candles,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"In the kitchen cabinet, Patrick. Do you know where the fuse box is?\" [401] \"Behind the dish cupboard, right? Okay, I'll see what I can do.\" [402] Miss Blacklock had already moved into the light from the restaurant. Dora Bunner gasped and choked back a sob, while Midge let out another piercing scream. [403] \"Blood, blood!\" she wailed. \"You're shot \u2013 Miss Blacklock, you're going to bleed to death.\" [404] \"Don't be silly,\" Miss Blacklock snapped. \"I'm not badly hurt, the bullet just grazed my ear.\" [405] \"But Aunt Letty,\" Julia said, \"all this blood.\" [406] Indeed, Miss Blacklock's blouse, pearl necklace, and hands were all covered in blood, looking rather ghastly. [407] \"Ears always bleed,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"I fainted in the barber's shop once when he cut my ear. Blood seemed to pour in a basin. But anyway, we must have light.\" [408] \"I'll get candles,\" Micky said. [409] Julia went with her, bringing back several candles stuck in dishes. [410] \"Now let's have a look at this villain,\" the Colonel said. \"Could you hold the candles lower, Swetnam? Bring as many as you can.\" [411] \"I'll go and light the other side,\" said Felicity. [412] She held two tea trays steady. The Colonel knelt down. [413] The prone figure wore a rough, hooded black cloak, a black mask over the face, and black cotton gloves. The hat had slipped back, revealing a tangled mass of golden hair. [414] Colonel Eastbrooke turned him over, feeling for a pulse, a heartbeat... and then, with great disgust, picked up his fingers and examined them closely. They were sticky and red. [415] \"He shot himself,\" he said. [416] \"Is he badly hurt?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."